(window.MAG=window.MAG||{}).d=(window.MAG.d||{});window.MAG.d["2-vatican-i"]=[["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p1","Session 1 — 8 December 1869","Pius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for an everlasting record.","Pius episcopus servus servorum Dei, sacro approbante concilio, ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p2","Session 1 — 8 December 1869","Most reverend fathers, does it please you that for the praise and glory of the holy and undivided Trinity, Father and Son and Holy Spirit, for the increase and exaltation of the catholic faith and religion, for the extirpation of spreading errors, for the reformation of the clergy and the Christian people, for the common peace and concord of all, a beginning be made of the sacred oecumenical Vatican council, and that it be declared to have been already made?","Reverendissimi patres, placet ne vobis ad laudem et gloriam sanctae et individuae Trinitatis, Patris et Filii et Spiritus sancti, ad incrementum et exaltationem fidei et religionis catholicae, ad extirpationem grassantium errorum, ad reformationem cleri populi que christiani, ad communem omnium pacem et concordiam sacrosancti oecumenici concilii Vaticani initium fieri, et iam factum esse declarari?"],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p3","Session 1 — 8 December 1869","Pius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for an everlasting record.","Pius episcopus servus servorum Dei, sacro approbante concilio, ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p4","Session 1 — 8 December 1869","Most reverend fathers, does it please you that the next session of the sacred oecumenical Vatican council be held on the feast of the Epiphany of the Lord, which day will be the sixth day of January of the year of the Lord 1870?","Reverendissimi patres, placet ne vobis proximam sacrosancti concilii oecumenici Vaticani sessionem haberi die festo epiphaniae Domini, qui dies erit sextus mensis ianuarii anno Domini 1870?"],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p5","Profession of Faith","I, Pius, bishop of the catholic church, with firm faith believe and profess all and each of the things which are contained in the symbol of faith which the holy Roman church uses, namely: I believe in one God, the Father almighty, maker of heaven and earth, of all things visible and invisible.","Ego Pius ecclesiae catholicae episcopus firma fide credo et profiteor omnia et singula, quae continentur in symbolo fidei, quo sancta Romana ecclesia utitur, videlicet: Credo in unum Deum Patrem omnipotentem, factorem caeli et terrae, visibilium omnium et invisibilium."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p6","Profession of Faith","And in one Lord Jesus Christ, the only-begotten Son of God.","Et in unum dominum Iesum Christum Filium Dei unigenitum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p7","Profession of Faith","And born of the Father before all ages.","Et ex Patre natum ante omnia saecula."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p8","Profession of Faith","God from God, light from light, true God from true God.","Deum de Deo, lumen de lumine, Deum verum de Deo vero."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p9","Profession of Faith","Begotten, not made, consubstantial with the Father: through whom all things were made.","Genitum, non factum, consubstantialem Patri: per quem omnia facta sunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p10","Profession of Faith","Who for us men and for our salvation descended from heaven.","Qui propter nos homines et propter nostram salutem descendit de caelis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p11","Profession of Faith","And was incarnate of the Holy Spirit from Mary the Virgin: and was made man.","Et incarnatus est de Spiritu sancto ex Maria virgine: et homo factus est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p12","Profession of Faith","He was also crucified for us, under Pontius Pilate suffered and was buried.","Crucifixus etiam pro nobis, sub Pontio Pilato passus et sepultus est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p13","Profession of Faith","And rose again on the third day, according to the scriptures.","Et resurrexit tertia die, secundum scripturas."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p14","Profession of Faith","And ascended into heaven, sits at the right hand of the Father.","Et ascendit in caelum, sedet ad dexteram Patris."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p15","Profession of Faith","And He is to come again with glory to judge the living and the dead, of whose kingdom there shall be no end.","Et iterum venturus est cum gloria iudicare vivos et mortuos, cuius regni non erit finis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p16","Profession of Faith","And in the Holy Spirit, the Lord and life-giver: who proceeds from the Father and the Son.","Et in Spiritum sanctum, dominum et vivificantem: qui ex Patre Filio que procedit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p17","Profession of Faith","Who together with the Father and the Son is adored and glorified: who spoke through the prophets.","Qui cum Patre et Filio simul adoratur et conglorificatur: qui locutus est per prophetas."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p18","Profession of Faith","And one, holy, catholic and apostolic church.","Et unam, sanctam, catholicam et apostolicam ecclesiam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p19","Profession of Faith","I confess one baptism for the remission of sins.","Confiteor unum baptisma in remissionem peccatorum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p20","Profession of Faith","And I await the resurrection of the dead.","Et expecto resurrectionem mortuorum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p21","Profession of Faith","And the life of the world to come. Amen.","Et vitam venturi saeculi. Amen."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p22","Profession of Faith","I most firmly admit and embrace the apostolic and ecclesiastical traditions and the other observances and constitutions of the same church.","Apostolicas et ecclesiasticas traditiones reliquas que eiusdem ecclesiae observationes et constitutiones firmissime admitto et amplector."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p23","Profession of Faith","Likewise, I admit sacred scripture according to that sense which holy mother church has held and holds, to whom it belongs to judge concerning the true sense and interpretation of the sacred scriptures, nor will I ever accept or interpret it except according to the unanimous consent of the fathers.","Item sacram scripturam iuxta eum sensum, quem tenuit et tenet sancta mater ecclesia, cuius est iudicare de vero sensu et interpretatione sacrarum scripturarum, admitto, nec eam unquam, nisi iuxta unanimem consensum patrum, accipiam et interpretabor."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p24","Profession of Faith","I profess likewise that there are seven truly and properly sacraments of the new law, instituted by Jesus Christ our Lord, and necessary for the salvation of the human race, although not all for each individual, namely: baptism, confirmation, eucharist, penance, extreme unction, order, and matrimony, and that they confer grace; and of these, baptism, confirmation, and order cannot be reiterated without sacrilege.","Profiteor quoque septem esse vere et proprie sacramenta novae legis a Iesu Christo domino nostro instituta, atque ad salutem humani generis, licet non omnia singulis, necessaria, scilicet baptismum, confirmationem eucharistiam, poenitentiam, extremam unctionem, ordinem et matrimonium, illa que gratiam conferre; et ex his baptismum, confirmationem et ordinem sine sacrilegio reiterari non posse."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p25","Profession of Faith","I also receive and admit the received and approved rites of the catholic church in the solemn administration of all the aforesaid sacraments.","Receptos quoque et adprobatos ecclesiae catholicae ritus in supradictorum omnium sacramentorum solemni administratione recipio et admitto."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p26","Profession of Faith","All and each of the things which have been defined and declared concerning original sin and justification in the most holy Tridentine synod, I embrace and receive.","Omnia et singula, quae de peccato originali et de iustificatione in sacrosancta Tridentina synodo definita et declarata fuerunt, amplector et recipio."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p27","Profession of Faith","I profess likewise that in the Mass a true, proper, and propitiatory sacrifice is offered to God for the living and the dead, and that in the most holy sacrament of the Eucharist there are truly, really, and substantially the body and blood together with the soul and divinity of our Lord Jesus Christ, and that a conversion is made of the whole substance of bread into the body, and of the whole substance of wine into the blood, which conversion the catholic church calls transubstantiation.","Profiteor pariter in missa offerri Deo verum, proprium et propitiatorium sacrificium pro vivis et defunctis, atque in sanctissimo eucharistiae sacramento esse vere, realiter et substantialiter corpus et sanguinem una cum anima et divinitate domini nostri Iesu Christi, fieri que conversionem totius substantiae panis in corpus, et totius substantiae vini in sanguinem, quam conversionem catholica ecclesia transsubstantiationem appellat."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p28","Profession of Faith","I confess also that under one species alone the whole and entire Christ and a true sacrament is received.","Fateor etiam sub altera tantum specie totum atque integrum Christum, verum que sacramentum sumi."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p29","Profession of Faith","I constantly hold that there is a purgatory, and that the souls detained there are helped by the suffrages of the faithful.","Constanter teneo purgatorium esse, animas que ibi detentas fidelium suffragiis iuvari."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p30","Profession of Faith","Likewise that the saints reigning together with Christ are to be venerated and invoked, and that they offer prayers to God for us, and that their relics are to be venerated.","Similiter et sanctos una cum Christo regnantes venerandos atque invocandos esse, eos que orationes Deo pro nobis offerre, atque eorum reliquias esse venerandas."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p31","Profession of Faith","I firmly assert that images of Christ and of the ever-virgin Mother of God, as well as of the other saints, are to be had and retained, and that due honor and veneration is to be imparted to them.","Firmiter assero imagines Christi ac Deiparae semper virginis, necnon aliorum sanctorum habendas et retinendas esse, atque eis debitum honorem ac venerationem impertiendam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p32","Profession of Faith","I affirm also that the power of indulgences was left by Christ in the church, and that their use is most salutary for the Christian people.","Indulgentiarum etiam potestatem a Christo in ecclesia relictam fuisse, illarum que usum christiano populo maxime salutarem esse affirmo."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p33","Profession of Faith","I acknowledge the holy, catholic, and apostolic Roman church as the mother and teacher of all churches.","Sanctam, catholicam et apostolicam Romanam ecclesiam omnium ecclesiarum matrem et magistram agnosco."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p34","Profession of Faith","All other things likewise handed down, defined, and declared by the sacred canons and oecumenical councils, and especially by the most holy Tridentine synod, I undoubtingly receive and profess; and at the same time all contrary things, and whatsoever heresies condemned, rejected, and anathematized by the church, I likewise condemn, reject, and anathematize.","Cetera item omnia a sacris canonibus et oecumenicis conciliis ac praecipue a sacrosancta Tridentina synodo tradita, definita ac declarata indubitanter recipio atque profiteor; simul que contraria omnia, atque haereses quascumque ab ecclesia damnatas et reiectas et anathematizatas ego pariter damno, reiicio et anathematizo."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p35","Profession of Faith","This true catholic faith, outside which no one can be saved, which at present I spontaneously profess and truly hold, the same whole and undefiled until the last breath of my life, I promise, vow, and swear most constantly, God helping, to retain and confess, and to take care, as far as in me lies, that it be held by all.","Hanc veram catholicam fidem, extra quam nemo salvus esse potest, quam in praesenti sponte profiteor et veraciter teneo, eamdem integram et immaculatam usque ad extremum vitae spiritum, constantissime, Deo adiuvante, retinere et confiteri, atque ab omnibus, quantum in me erit, curaturum ego idem Pius spondeo, voveo ac iuro."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p36","Profession of Faith","So help me God and these holy gospels of God.","Sic me Deus adiuvet et haec sancta Dei evangelia."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p37","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Pius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for an everlasting record.","Pius episcopus servus servorum dei sacro approbante concilio ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p38","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","The Son of God and the Redeemer of the human race, our Lord Jesus Christ, about to return to the heavenly Father, promised that He would be with His church militant on earth all days, even to the consummation of the age. Wherefore He never ceased to be present to His beloved spouse, to assist her when teaching, to bless her when working, to bring help to her when in peril.","Dei filius et generis humani redemptor dominus noster Iesus Christus ad patrem caelestem rediturus cum ecclesia sua in terris militante omnibus diebus usque ad consummationem saeculi futurum se esse promisit. Quare dilectae sponsae praesto esse adsistere docenti operanti benedicere periclitanti opem ferre nullo unquam tempore destitit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p39","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","This salutary providence of His has continually appeared from other innumerable benefits, and has been most manifestly discovered by those abundant fruits which have issued to the Christian world from oecumenical councils, and especially from the council of Trent, although celebrated in evil times.","Haec vero salutaris eius providentia cum ex aliis beneficiis innumeris continenter apparuit tum iis manifestissime comperta est fructibus qui orbi christiano e conciliis oecumenicis ac nominatim e Tridentino iniquis licet temporibus celebrato amplissimi provenerunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p40","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","For from it the most holy dogmas of religion have been more precisely defined and more abundantly set forth; errors have been condemned and restrained; ecclesiastical discipline has been restored and more firmly sanctioned; the study of knowledge and piety in the clergy has been promoted; colleges for educating youth for the sacred warfare have been provided; finally, the morals of the Christian people have been renewed both by more accurate instruction of the faithful and by more frequent use of the sacraments.","Hinc enim sanctissima religionis dogmata pressius definita uberius que exposita, errores damnati atque cohibiti, hinc ecclesiastica disciplina restituta firmius que sancita, promotum in clero scientiae et pietatis studium, parata adolescentibus ad sacram militiam educandis collegia, christiani denique populi mores et accuratiore fidelium eruditione et frequentiore sacramentorum usu instaurati."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p41","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","From it moreover there has resulted a closer communion of the members with the visible head, and an added vigor to the whole mystical body of Christ; from it religious families have been multiplied and other institutes of Christian piety; from it also that constant ardor, even to the shedding of blood, for propagating the kingdom of Christ far and wide.","Hinc praeterea arctior membrorum cum visibili capite communio universo que corpori Christi mystico additus vigor, hinc religiosae multiplicatae familiae alia que christianae pietatis instituta, hinc ille etiam assiduus et usque ad sanguinis effusionem constans ardor in Christi regno late per orbem propagando."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p42","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Nevertheless, while we recall these and other notable advantages which divine clemency has bestowed upon the church, especially through the last oecumenical synod, with the grateful mind that is fitting, we cannot restrain our bitter grief on account of the most grievous evils which have arisen chiefly because the authority of the same sacred synod has been despised or its most wise decrees have been neglected by very many.","Verumtamen haec alia que insignia emolumenta quae per ultimam maxime oecumenicam synodum divina clementia ecclesiae largita est dum grato quo par est animo recolimus acerbum compescere haud possumus dolorem ob mala gravissima inde potissimum orta quod eiusdem sacrosanctae synodi apud permultos vel auctoritas contempta vel sapientissima neglecta fuere decreta."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p43","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","For no one is ignorant that the heresies which the fathers of Trent proscribed, when the divine magisterium of the church was rejected and matters pertaining to religion were left to the judgment of each individual, have gradually been dissolved into many sects, which, disagreeing and contending among themselves, have finally caused all faith in Christ to be shaken among not a few.","Nemo enim ignorat haereses quas Tridentini patres proscripserunt dum reiecto divino ecclesiae magisterio res ad religionem spectantes privati cuiusvis iudicio permitterentur in sectas paullatim dissolutas esse multiplices quibus inter se dissentientibus et concertantibus omnis tandem in Christum fides apud non paucos labefactata est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p44","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Thus the sacred scriptures themselves, which before were asserted to be the sole source and judge of Christian doctrine, have now begun to be held not as divine, indeed to be accounted among mythical commentaries.","Itaque ipsa sacra biblia quae antea christianae doctrinae unicus fons et iudex asserebantur iam non pro divinis haberi, imo mythicis commentis accenseri coeperunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p45","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Then was born and spread far too widely through the world that doctrine of rationalism or naturalism, which, opposing itself in every way to the Christian religion as a supernatural institution, strives with the greatest effort that, Christ (who alone is our Lord and Savior) having been excluded from the minds of men and from the life and morals of nations, the kingdom of what they call mere reason or nature may be established.","Tum nata est et late nimis per orbem vagata illa rationalismi seu naturalismi doctrina quae religioni christianae utpote supernaturali instituto per omnia adversans summo studio molitur ut Christo qui solus dominus et salvator noster est a mentibus humanis a vita et moribus populorum excluso merae quod vocant rationis vel naturae regnum stabiliatur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p46","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","The Christian religion having been abandoned and rejected, and the true God and His Christ having been denied, the mind of many has at length sunk into the pit of pantheism, materialism, and atheism, so that now, denying even rational nature and every norm of justice and right, they strive to tear down the deepest foundations of human society.","Relicta autem proiecta que christiana religione, negato vero deo et Christo eius, prolapsa tandem est multorum mens in pantheismi, materialismi, atheismi barathrum, ut iam ipsam rationalem naturam omnem que iusti recti que normam negantes ima humanae societatis fundamenta diruere connitantur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p47","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Furthermore, with this impiety raging on all sides, it has unfortunately come to pass that many even of the children of the catholic church have wandered from the way of true piety, and, the truths of faith being gradually diminished in them, the catholic sense has been weakened.","Hac porro impietate circumquaque grassante infeliciter contigit ut plures etiam e catholicae ecclesiae filiis a via verae pietatis aberrarent, in iis que diminutis paullatim veritatibus sensus catholicus attenuaretur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p48","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","For, led away by various and strange doctrines, wrongly mixing nature and grace, human knowledge and divine faith, they are found to deprave the genuine sense of the dogmas which holy mother church holds and teaches, and to bring the integrity and sincerity of the faith into peril.","Variis enim ac peregrinis doctrinis abducti naturam et gratiam, scientiam humanam et fidem divinam perperam commiscentes, genuinum sensum dogmatum quem tenet ac docet sancta mater ecclesia depravare, integritatem que et sinceritatem fidei in periculum adducere comperiuntur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p49","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","All these things being considered, how can it be that the inmost bowels of the church are not moved?","Quibus omnibus perspectis fieri qui potest ut non commoveantur intima ecclesiae viscera."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p50","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","For just as God wills all men to be saved and to come to the knowledge of the truth, just as Christ came to save what was lost and to gather into one the children of God who were scattered abroad, so the church, constituted by God as the mother and teacher of nations, knowing herself to be a debtor to all, is always ready and intent to raise up the fallen, to sustain those who waver, to embrace those who return, to confirm the good, and to lead them forward to better things.","Quemadmodum enim deus vult omnes homines salvos fieri et ad agnitionem veritatis venire, quemadmodum Christus venit ut salvum faceret quod perierat et filios dei qui erant dispersi congregaret in unum, ita ecclesia a deo populorum mater et magistra constituta omnibus debitricem se novit ac lapsos erigere, labantes sustinere, revertentes amplecti, confirmare bonos et ad meliora provehere parata semper et intenta est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p51","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Wherefore at no time can she rest from testifying and proclaiming the truth of God which heals all things, knowing that it was said to her: \"My Spirit who is in you, and my words which I have put in your mouth, shall not depart out of your mouth, from henceforth and forever.\"","Quapropter nullo tempore a dei veritate quae sanat omnia testanda et praedicanda quiescere potest, sibi dictum esse non ignorans: \"spiritus meus qui est in te et verba mea quae posui in ore tuo non recedent de ore tuo amodo et usque in sempiternum.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p52","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","We therefore, adhering to the footsteps of our predecessors, have never ceased, according to our supreme apostolic office, to teach and defend the catholic truth and to reprove perverse doctrines.","Nos itaque inhaerentes praedecessorum nostrorum vestigiis pro supremo nostro apostolico munere veritatem catholicam docere ac tueri perversas que doctrinas reprobare nunquam intermisimus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p53","Session 2 — 6 January 1870","Now, sitting with us and judging with us the bishops of the whole world, gathered by our authority in the Holy Spirit into this oecumenical council, resting on the word of God written and handed down as we have received it from the catholic church, holily preserved and genuinely set forth, we have determined from this chair of Peter in the sight of all to profess and declare the salutary doctrine of Christ, the adverse errors having been proscribed and condemned by the power given us by God.","Nunc autem sedentibus nobis cum et iudicantibus universi orbis episcopis in hanc oecumenicam synodum auctoritate nostra in spiritu sancto congregatis innixi dei verbo scripto et tradito, prout ab ecclesia catholica sancte custoditum et genuine expositum accepimus, ex hac Petri cathedra in conspectu omnium salutarem Christi doctrinam profiteri et declarare constituimus, adversis erroribus potestate nobis a deo tradita proscriptis atque damnatis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p54","Chapter 1","The holy, catholic, apostolic, Roman church believes and confesses that there is one true and living God, creator and lord of heaven and earth, almighty, eternal, immense, incomprehensible, infinite in intellect and will and in every perfection; who, since He is one, singular, altogether simple and unchangeable spiritual substance, is to be proclaimed as really and essentially distinct from the world, most blessed in Himself and from Himself, and ineffably exalted above all things which exist besides Him or can be conceived.","Sancta catholica apostolica Romana ecclesia credit et confitetur unum esse deum verum et vivum creatorem ac dominum caeli et terrae omnipotentem aeternum immensum incomprehensibilem intellectu ac voluntate omni que perfectione infinitum; qui cum sit una singularis simplex omnino et incommutabilis substantia spiritualis, praedicandus est re et essentia a mundo distinctus, in se et ex se beatissimus et super omnia quae praeter ipsum sunt et concipi possunt ineffabiliter excelsus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p55","Chapter 1","This one true God, by His goodness and almighty power, not to increase His own beatitude nor to acquire it, but to manifest His perfection through the goods which He bestows on creatures, by the most free counsel, simultaneously from the beginning of time, founded both creations from nothing, the spiritual and the corporal, namely angelic and worldly, and then human, as it were common, constituted from spirit and body.","Hic solus verus deus bonitate sua et omnipotenti virtute non ad augendam suam beatitudinem nec ad acquirendam sed ad manifestandam perfectionem suam per bona quae creaturis impertitur liberrimo consilio simul ab initio temporis utramque de nihilo condidit creaturam spiritualem et corporalem, angelicam videlicet et mundanam, ac deinde humanam quasi communem ex spiritu et corpore constitutam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p56","Chapter 1","But God protects and governs by His providence all things which He has founded, reaching from end to end mightily and ordering all things sweetly. \"For all things are naked and open to His eyes,\" even those which are to be done by the free action of creatures.","Universa vero quae condidit deus providentia sua tuetur atque gubernat, attingens a fine usque ad finem fortiter et disponens omnia suaviter. \"Omnia enim nuda et aperta sunt oculis eius,\" ea etiam quae libera creaturarum actione futura sunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p57","Chapter 2","The same holy mother church holds and teaches that God, the beginning and end of all things, can be known with certainty from created things by the natural light of human reason; for \"the invisible things of Him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made.\" Nevertheless, it pleased His wisdom and goodness to reveal Himself and the eternal decrees of His will to the human race by another, that is, a supernatural way, the apostle saying: \"God, who at sundry times and in divers manners spoke in times past to the fathers by the prophets, last of all in these days has spoken to us by His Son.\"","Eadem sancta mater ecclesia tenet et docet deum rerum omnium principium et finem naturali humanae rationis lumine e rebus creatis certo cognosci posse; \"invisibilia enim ipsius a creatura mundi per ea quae facta sunt intellecta conspiciuntur.\" Attamen placuisse eius sapientiae et bonitati alia ea que supernaturali via se ipsum ac aeterna voluntatis suae decreta humano generi revelare, dicente apostolo: \"Multifariam multis que modis olim deus loquens patribus in prophetis, novissime diebus istis locutus est nobis in filio.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p58","Chapter 2","To this divine revelation it is indeed to be attributed that those things which in divine things are not of themselves inaccessible to human reason can, even in the present condition of the human race, be known by all with ease, with firm certainty, and with no admixture of error. Not, however, for this reason is revelation to be said absolutely necessary; but because God, out of His infinite goodness, has ordained man to a supernatural end, namely, to partake of divine goods which altogether surpass the understanding of the human mind; for \"eye has not seen, nor ear heard, neither has it entered into the heart of man, what things God has prepared for those who love Him.\"","Huic divinae revelationi tribuendum quidem est ut ea quae in rebus divinis humanae rationi per se impervia non sunt in praesenti quoque generis humani conditione ab omnibus expedite, firma certitudine et nullo admixto errore cognosci possint. Non hac tamen de causa revelatio absolute necessaria dicenda est, sed quia deus ex infinita bonitate sua ordinavit hominem ad finem supernaturalem, ad participanda scilicet bona divina quae humanae mentis intelligentiam omnino superant; siquidem \"oculus non vidit nec auris audivit nec in cor hominis ascendit quae praeparavit deus iis qui diligunt illum.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p59","Chapter 3","Since man wholly depends on God as his Creator and Lord, and created reason is altogether subject to uncreated truth, we are bound to yield to God, when revealing, the full obedience of intellect and will, by faith.","Cum homo a deo tanquam creatore et domino suo totus dependeat et ratio creata increatae veritati penitus subiecta sit, plenum revelanti deo intellectus et voluntatis obsequium fide praestare tenemur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p60","Chapter 3","This faith, which the catholic church professes, is a supernatural virtue, by which, with the inspiration and assistance of God's grace, we believe that the things revealed by Him are true, not because the intrinsic truth of the things is perceived by the natural light of reason, but because of the authority of God Himself who reveals, who can neither be deceived nor deceive.","Hanc vero fidem quae humanae salutis initium est ecclesia catholica profitetur virtutem esse supernaturalem, qua dei aspirante et adiuvante gratia ab eo revelata vera esse credimus, non propter intrinsecam rerum veritatem naturali rationis lumine perspectam, sed propter auctoritatem ipsius dei revelantis, qui nec falli nec fallere potest."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p61","Chapter 3","For faith, as the apostle testifies, \"is the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen.\"","Est enim fides, testante apostolo, \"sperandarum substantia rerum, argumentum non apparentium.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p62","Chapter 3","Nevertheless, that the obedience of our faith might be consistent with reason, God has willed that to the internal helps of the Holy Spirit there should be joined external arguments of His revelation, namely, divine facts, and especially miracles and prophecies, which, since they clearly show forth the omnipotence and infinite knowledge of God, are most certain signs of divine revelation, accommodated to the understanding of all.","Ut nihilominus fidei nostrae obsequium rationi consentaneum esset, voluit deus cum internis spiritus sancti auxiliis externa iungi revelationis suae argumenta, facta scilicet divina atque imprimis miracula et prophetias, quae cum dei omnipotentiam et infinitam scientiam luculenter commonstrent, divinae revelationis signa sunt certissima et omnium intelligentiae accommodata."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p63","Chapter 3","Wherefore both Moses and the prophets, and especially Christ the Lord Himself, wrought many and most evident miracles and prophecies; and of the apostles we read: \"They going forth preached everywhere, the Lord working withal and confirming the word with signs that followed.\" And again it is written: \"We have the more firm prophetical word, to which you do well to attend, as to a light shining in a dark place.\"","Quare tum Moyses et prophetae, tum ipse maxime Christus dominus multa et manifestissima miracula et prophetias ediderunt; et de apostolis legimus: \"illi autem profecti praedicaverunt ubique, domino cooperante et sermonem confirmante sequentibus signis.\" Et rursum scriptum est: \"habemus firmiorem propheticum sermonem, cui bene facitis attendentes quasi lucernae lucenti in caliginoso loco.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p64","Chapter 3","But although the assent of faith is by no means a blind motion of the mind, yet no one can consent to the gospel preaching as he ought for obtaining salvation without the illumination and inspiration of the Holy Spirit, who gives to all the sweetness of consenting to and believing the truth.","Licet autem fidei assensus nequaquam sit motus animi caecus, nemo tamen evangelicae praedicationi consentire potest sicut oportet ad salutem consequendam absque illuminatione et inspiratione spiritus sancti, qui dat omnibus suavitatem in consentiendo et credendo veritati."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p65","Chapter 3","Wherefore faith itself, even if it does not operate through charity, is a gift of God, and its act is a work pertaining to salvation, by which man renders to God Himself free obedience, consenting to His grace and cooperating with it, which he could resist.","Quare fides ipsa in se, etiamsi per charitatem non operetur, donum dei est, et actus eius est opus ad salutem pertinens, quo homo liberam praestat ipsi deo obedientiam gratiae eius, cui resistere posset, consentiendo et cooperando."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p66","Chapter 3","Moreover, all those things are to be believed by divine and catholic faith which are contained in the word of God, written or handed down, and are proposed by the church as divinely revealed, either by a solemn judgment or by the ordinary and universal magisterium.","Porro fide divina et catholica ea omnia credenda sunt quae in verbo dei scripto vel tradito continentur et ab ecclesia sive solemni iudicio sive ordinario et universali magisterio tamquam divinitus revelata credenda proponuntur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p67","Chapter 3","But since without faith it is impossible to please God and to come to the fellowship of His children, therefore no one ever attained justification without it, nor shall anyone obtain eternal life unless he shall have persevered in it until the end.","Quoniam vero sine fide impossibile est placere deo et ad filiorum eius consortium pervenire, ideo nemini unquam sine illa contigit iustificatio, nec ullus nisi in ea perseveraverit usque in finem vitam aeternam assequetur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p68","Chapter 4","Moreover, the catholic church has always held and holds that there is a twofold order of knowledge, distinct not only in principle but also in object; in principle, because in one we know by natural reason, in the other by divine faith; in object, because besides those things to which natural reason can attain, mysteries hidden in God are proposed to us for belief, which, unless they are divinely revealed, cannot become known.","Hoc quoque perpetuus ecclesiae catholicae consensus tenuit et tenet duplicem esse ordinem cognitionis, non solum principio sed obiecto etiam distinctum; principio quidem quia in altero naturali ratione, in altero fide divina cognoscimus; obiecto autem quia praeter ea ad quae naturalis ratio pertingere potest, credenda nobis proponuntur mysteria in deo abscondita, quae nisi revelata divinitus innotescere non possunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p69","Chapter 4","Wherefore the apostle, who testifies that God is known to the gentiles through created things, yet when he treats of the grace and truth which came through Jesus Christ, says: \"We speak the wisdom of God in a mystery, which is hidden, which God ordained before the world unto our glory, which none of the princes of this world knew.\" And the only-begotten Himself confesses to the Father \"because You have hidden these things from the wise and prudent, and have revealed them to the little ones.\"","Quocirca apostolus, qui a gentibus deum per ea quae facta sunt cognitum esse testatur, disserens tamen de gratia et veritate quae per Iesum Christum facta est, pronuntiat: \"loquimur dei sapientiam in mysterio, quae abscondita est, quam praedestinavit deus ante saecula in gloriam nostram, quam nemo principum huius saeculi cognovit.\" Et ipse unigenitus confitetur patri: \"quia abscondisti haec a sapientibus et prudentibus et revelasti ea parvulis.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p70","Chapter 4","But reason, illustrated by faith, when it seeks assiduously, piously, and soberly, attains by the gift of God some understanding of the mysteries, most fruitful, both from the analogy of those things which it naturally knows and from the connection of the mysteries themselves with one another and with the ultimate end of man; never, however, is it rendered capable of perceiving them as those truths which constitute its proper object. For the divine mysteries of their own nature so transcend created intellect that, even when handed down by revelation and received by faith, they remain covered with the veil of faith itself and shrouded in a certain obscurity, as long as in this mortal life we are absent from the Lord; \"for we walk by faith, and not by sight.\"","Ac ratio quidem fide illustrata, cum sedulo, pie et sobrie quaerit, aliquam deo dante mysteriorum intelligentiam eam que fructuosissimam assequitur, tum ex eorum quae naturaliter cognoscit analogia, tum e mysteriorum ipsorum nexu inter se et cum fine hominis ultimo; nunquam tamen idonea redditur ad ea perspicienda instar veritatum quae proprium ipsius obiectum constituunt. Divina enim mysteria suapte natura intellectum creatum sic excedunt ut, etiam revelatione tradita et fide suscepta, ipsius tamen fidei velamine contecta et quadam quasi caligine obvoluta maneant, quamdiu in hac mortali vita peregrinamur a domino; \"per fidem enim ambulamus, et non per speciem.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p71","Chapter 4","But although faith is above reason, no true dissension can ever exist between faith and reason, since the same God who reveals mysteries and infuses faith has placed the light of reason in the human mind; and God cannot deny Himself, nor can truth ever contradict truth.","Verum etsi fides sit supra rationem, nulla tamen unquam inter fidem et rationem vera dissensio esse potest, cum idem deus qui mysteria revelat et fidem infundit animo humano rationis lumen indiderit; deus autem negare se ipsum non possit, nec verum vero unquam contradicere."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p72","Chapter 4","The vain appearance of such contradiction arises chiefly from this: that either the dogmas of faith are not understood and set forth according to the mind of the church, or that the inventions of opinion are held as the dictates of reason.","Inanis autem huius contradictionis species inde potissimum oritur quod vel fidei dogmata ad mentem ecclesiae intellecta et exposita non fuerint, vel opinionum commenta pro rationis effatis habeantur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p73","Chapter 4","Therefore we define that every assertion contrary to the truth of illuminated faith is altogether false.","Omnem igitur assertionem veritati illuminatae fidei contrariam omnino falsam esse definimus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p74","Chapter 4","Moreover, the church, which together with the apostolic office of teaching has received the mandate of guarding the deposit of faith, has also divinely the right and duty of proscribing knowledge falsely so called, lest any be deceived by philosophy and vain deceit.","Porro ecclesia, quae una cum apostolico munere docendi mandatum accepit fidei depositum custodiendi, ius etiam et officium divinitus habet falsi nominis scientiam proscribendi, ne quis decipiatur per philosophiam et inanem fallaciam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p75","Chapter 4","Wherefore all Christian faithful are not only forbidden to defend as legitimate conclusions of science those opinions which are known to be contrary to the doctrine of faith, especially when condemned by the church, but are bound altogether to hold them as errors which bear a false appearance of truth.","Quapropter omnes christiani fideles huiusmodi opiniones quae fidei doctrinae contrariae esse cognoscuntur, maxime si ab ecclesia reprobatae fuerint, non solum prohibentur tanquam legitimas scientiae conclusiones defendere, sed pro erroribus potius qui fallacem veritatis speciem prae se ferant, habere tenentur omnino."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p76","Chapter 4","Nor can faith and reason ever dissent from each other; but they mutually assist each other, since right reason demonstrates the foundations of faith and, illuminated by its light, cultivates the science of divine things, while faith frees reason from errors and protects it and instructs it with manifold knowledge.","Neque solum fides et ratio inter se dissidere nunquam possunt, sed opem quoque sibi mutuam ferunt, cum recta ratio fidei fundamenta demonstret, eius que lumine illustrata rerum divinarum scientiam excolat, fides vero rationem ab erroribus liberet ac tueatur eam que multiplici cognitione instruat."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p77","Chapter 4","Wherefore so far is the church from hindering the culture of human arts and disciplines, that she in many ways aids and promotes it. For she does not ignore nor despise the advantages which flow from them to the life of men; indeed she confesses that they, as they have proceeded from God the Lord of sciences, so if they be rightly handled, lead to God by the help of His grace.","Quapropter tantum abest ut ecclesia humanarum artium et disciplinarum culturae obsistat, ut hanc multis modis iuvet atque promoveat. Non enim commoda ab iis ad hominum vitam dimanantia aut ignorat aut despicit, fatetur imo eas, quemadmodum a deo scientiarum domino profectae sunt, ita si rite pertractentur, ad deum iuvante eius gratia perducere."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p78","Chapter 4","Nor does she forbid that each of these disciplines in its own sphere use its own principles and its own method; but recognizing this just liberty, she takes care that they may not, by opposing divine doctrine, take errors into themselves, or, having transgressed their own boundaries, occupy and disturb those things which are of faith.","Nec sane ipsa vetat ne huiusmodi disciplinae in suo quaeque ambitu propriis utantur principiis et propria methodo, sed iustam hanc libertatem agnoscens, id sedulo cavet ne divinae doctrinae repugnando errores in se suscipiant, aut fines proprios transgressae ea quae sunt fidei occupent et perturbent."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p79","Chapter 4","For the doctrine of faith which God has revealed has not been proposed as a philosophical invention to be perfected by human ingenuity, but as a divine deposit handed down to the spouse of Christ, to be faithfully guarded and infallibly declared.","Neque enim fidei doctrina quam deus revelavit velut philosophicum inventum proposita est humanis ingeniis perficienda, sed tanquam divinum depositum Christi sponsae tradita, fideliter custodienda et infallibiliter declaranda."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p80","Chapter 4","Hence also that sense of the sacred dogmas is perpetually to be retained which holy mother church has once declared, nor is it ever to be departed from under the pretense and name of a higher understanding.","Hinc sacrorum quoque dogmatum is sensus perpetuo est retinendus quem semel declaravit sancta mater ecclesia, nec unquam ab eo sensu altioris intelligentiae specie et nomine recedendum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p81","Chapter 4","\"Let intelligence, knowledge, and wisdom increase therefore, and greatly and vehemently, both of individuals and of all, of one man and of the whole church, in the course of ages and centuries, but only in its own kind, namely, in the same dogma, in the same sense, and in the same meaning.\"","\"Crescat igitur et multum vehementer que proficiat tam singulorum quam omnium, tam unius hominis quam totius ecclesiae, aetatum ac saeculorum gradibus intelligentia, scientia, sapientia, sed in suo dumtaxat genere, in eodem scilicet dogmate, eodem sensu eadem que sententia.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p82","Canons on God the Creator of All Things and on the Catholic Faith","If anyone denies the one true God, the creator and lord of visible and invisible things: let him be anathema.","Si quis unum verum deum visibilium et invisibilium creatorem et dominum negaverit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p83","Canon 2","If anyone does not blush to affirm that matter alone exists: let him be anathema.","Si quis praeter materiam nihil esse affirmare non erubuerit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p84","Canon 3","If anyone says that the substance or essence of God and of all things is one and the same: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit unam eandem que esse dei et rerum omnium substantiam vel essentiam: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p85","Canon 4","If anyone says that finite things, both corporeal and spiritual, or at least spiritual, have emanated from the divine substance, or that the divine essence by its manifestation or evolution becomes all things, or finally that God is universal or indefinite being which by determining itself constitutes the universe of things distinct into genera, species, and individuals: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit res finitas, tum corporeas tum spirituales, aut saltem spirituales, e divina substantia emanasse, aut divinam essentiam sui manifestatione vel evolutione fieri omnia, aut denique deum esse ens universale seu indefinitum quod sese determinando constituat rerum universitatem in genera, species et individua distinctam: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p86","Canon 5","If anyone does not confess that the world and all things which are contained in it, both spiritual and material, according to their whole substance, have been produced by God from nothing; or says that God created not by His will free from all necessity, but as necessarily as He necessarily loves Himself; or denies that the world was created for the glory of God: let him be anathema.","Si quis non confiteatur mundum res que omnes quae in eo continentur, et spirituales et materiales, secundum totam suam substantiam, a deo ex nihilo esse productas; aut deum dixerit non voluntate ab omni necessitate libera, sed tam necessario creasse quam necessario amat se ipsum; aut mundum ad dei gloriam conditum esse negaverit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p87","Canon 6","If anyone says that the one and true God our creator and lord cannot be certainly known by the natural light of human reason through created things: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit deum unum et verum creatorem et dominum nostrum per ea quae facta sunt naturali rationis humanae lumine certo cognosci non posse: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p88","Canon 7","If anyone says that it is impossible or inexpedient that man be taught by divine revelation concerning God and the worship to be shown to Him: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit fieri non posse aut non expedire ut per revelationem divinam homo de deo cultu que ei exhibendo edoceatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p89","Canon 8","If anyone says that man cannot be divinely raised to a knowledge and perfection which surpasses the natural, but that from himself he can and ought to attain by continual progress to the possession of all truth and goodness: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit hominem ad cognitionem et perfectionem quae naturalem superet divinitus evehi non posse, sed ex se ipso ad omnis tandem veri et boni possessionem iugi profectu pertingere posse et debere: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p90","Canon 9","If anyone does not receive the books of sacred scripture in their entirety, with all their parts, as the holy Tridentine synod enumerated them, as sacred and canonical, or denies that they are divinely inspired: let him be anathema.","Si quis sacrae scripturae libros integros cum omnibus suis partibus, prout illos sancta Tridentina synodus recensuit, pro sacris et canonicis non susceperit, aut eos divinitus inspiratos esse negaverit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p91","Canon 10","If anyone says that human reason is so independent that faith cannot be commanded to it by God: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit rationem humanam ita independentem esse ut fides ei a deo imperari non possit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p92","Canon 11","If anyone says that divine faith is not distinguished from natural knowledge of God and of moral truths, and therefore that for divine faith it is not required that revealed truth be believed because of the authority of God who reveals: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit fidem divinam a naturali de deo et rebus moralibus scientia non distingui, ac propterea ad fidem divinam non requiri ut revelata veritas propter auctoritatem dei revelantis credatur: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p93","Canon 12","If anyone says that divine revelation cannot be made credible by external signs, and therefore that men ought to be moved to faith only by the internal experience of each or by private inspiration: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit revelationem divinam externis signis credibilem fieri non posse, ideo que sola interna cuiusque experientia aut inspiratione privata homines ad fidem moveri debere: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p94","Canon 13","If anyone says that miracles cannot be wrought, and therefore that all narrations of them, even those contained in sacred scripture, are to be relegated among fables or myths, or that miracles can never be certainly known, nor can the divine origin of the Christian religion be rightly proved by them: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit miracula nulla fieri posse, proinde que omnes de iis narrationes, etiam in sacra scriptura contentas, inter fabulas vel mythos ablegandas esse, aut miracula certo cognosci nunquam posse, nec iis divinam religionis christianae originem rite probari: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p95","Canon 14","If anyone says that the assent of Christian faith is not free, but is necessarily produced by arguments of human reason; or that to living faith alone, which works through charity, is the grace of God necessary: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit assensum fidei christianae non esse liberum, sed argumentis humanae rationis necessario produci; aut ad solam fidem vivam quae per charitatem operatur gratiam dei necessariam esse: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p96","Canon 15","If anyone says that the condition of the faithful and of those who have not yet attained to the only true faith is equal, so that catholics can have just cause for calling into doubt with suspended assent the faith which they have already received under the magisterium of the church, until they shall have completed a scientific demonstration of the credibility and truth of their faith: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit parem esse conditionem fidelium atque eorum qui ad fidem unice veram nondum pervenerunt, ita ut catholici iustam causam habere possint fidem quam sub ecclesiae magisterio iam susceperunt assensu suspenso in dubium vocandi, donec demonstrationem scientificam credibilitatis et veritatis fidei suae absolverint: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p97","Canon 16","If anyone says that in divine revelation there are no true and properly so-called mysteries, but that all the dogmas of faith can be understood and demonstrated by reason rightly cultivated from natural principles: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit in revelatione divina nulla vera et proprie dicta mysteria contineri, sed universa fidei dogmata posse per rationem rite excultam e naturalibus principiis intelligi et demonstrari: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p98","Canon 17","If anyone says that human disciplines are to be treated with such freedom that their assertions, even if opposed to revealed doctrine, can be held as true, and cannot be proscribed by the church: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit disciplinas humanas ea cum libertate tractandas esse ut earum assertiones, etsi doctrinae revelatae adversentur, tanquam verae retineri neque ab ecclesia proscribi possint: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p99","Canon 18","If anyone says that it is possible that at some time, according to the progress of science, a sense is to be given to the dogmas proposed by the church different from that which the church has understood and understands: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit fieri posse ut dogmatibus ab ecclesia propositis aliquando secundum progressum scientiae sensus tribuendus sit alius ab eo quem intellexit et intelligit ecclesia: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p100","Canon 18","Therefore, fulfilling the duty of our supreme pastoral office, we adjure all faithful of Christ, especially those who preside or who exercise the office of teaching, through the bowels of Jesus Christ, and we command by the authority of the same God and our Savior, that they contribute their study and labor to warding off and eliminating these errors from holy church and to spreading the light of the purest faith.","Itaque supremi pastoralis nostri officii debitum exequentes, omnes Christi fideles, maxime vero eos qui praesunt vel docendi munere funguntur, per viscera Iesu Christi obtestamur, nec non eiusdem dei et salvatoris nostri auctoritate iubemus, ut ad hos errores a sancta ecclesia arcendos et eliminandos atque purissimae fidei lucem pandendam studium et operam conferant."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p101","Canon 18","But since it is not enough to avoid heretical depravity unless those errors also be diligently shunned which more or less approach it, we warn all of the duty of observing also the constitutions and decrees by which such evil opinions (which are not explicitly enumerated here) have been proscribed and prohibited by this holy see.","Quoniam vero satis non est haereticam pravitatem devitare, nisi ii quoque errores diligenter fugiantur qui ad illam plus minus ve accedunt, omnes officii monemus servandi etiam constitutiones et decreta quibus pravae eiusmodi opiniones (quae isthic diserte non enumerantur) ab hac sancta sede proscriptae et prohibitae sunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p102","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","Pius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, with the sacred council approving, for an everlasting record.","Pius episcopus servus servorum dei sacro approbante concilio ad perpetuam rei memoriam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p103","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","The eternal Shepherd and Bishop of our souls, in order to render the saving work of redemption perpetual, decreed to build a holy church, in which, as in the house of the living God, all the faithful might be contained by the bond of one faith and charity.","Pastor aeternus et episcopus animarum nostrarum, ut salutiferum redemptionis opus perenne redderet, sanctam aedificare ecclesiam decrevit, in qua veluti in domo dei viventis fideles omnes unius fidei et charitatis vinculo continerentur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p104","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","Wherefore before He was glorified, He prayed the Father not for the apostles only, but also for those who should believe in Him through their word, that all might be one, as He Himself the Son and the Father are one.","Quapropter priusquam clarificaretur, rogavit patrem non pro apostolis tantum, sed et pro eis qui credituri erant per verbum eorum in ipsum, ut omnes unum essent, sicut ipse filius et pater unum sunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p105","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","Therefore, just as He sent the apostles whom He had chosen from the world, as He Himself had been sent by the Father, so He willed that there should be pastors and teachers in His church even to the consummation of the age.","Quemadmodum igitur apostolos, quos sibi de mundo elegerat, misit sicut ipse missus erat a patre, ita in ecclesia sua pastores et doctores usque ad consummationem saeculi esse voluit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p106","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","But that the episcopate itself might be one and undivided, and that the whole multitude of believers might be preserved in the unity of faith and communion through the priests who cohere together, He placed blessed Peter before the other apostles, and established in him the perpetual principle and visible foundation of both unities, upon whose strength the eternal temple should be built, and the sublime height of the church to be raised to heaven should rise in the firmness of this faith.","Ut vero episcopatus ipse unus et indivisus esset et per cohaerentes sibi invicem sacerdotes credentium multitudo universa in fidei et communionis unitate conservaretur, beatum Petrum caeteris apostolis praeponens, in ipso instituit perpetuum utriusque unitatis principium ac visibile fundamentum, super cuius fortitudinem aeternum extrueretur templum et ecclesiae caelo inferenda sublimitas in huius fidei firmitate consurgeret."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p107","Dogmatic Constitution on the Church of Christ","And since the gates of hell, rising up with greater hatred each day against the divinely placed foundation to overthrow if possible the church, we, for the protection, safety, and increase of the catholic flock, have judged it necessary, with the sacred council approving, to set forth to all the faithful to be believed and held, according to the ancient and constant faith of the universal church, the doctrine concerning the institution, perpetuity, and nature of the sacred apostolic primacy, in which the strength and solidity of the whole church consists, and to proscribe and condemn the contrary errors, so pernicious to the Lord's flock.","Et quoniam portae inferi, ad evertendam si fieri posset ecclesiam contra eius fundamentum divinitus positum maiori in dies odio undique insurgunt, nos ad catholici gregis custodiam, incolumitatem, augmentum necessarium esse iudicamus, sacro approbante concilio, doctrinam de institutione, perpetuitate ac natura sacri apostolici primatus, in quo totius ecclesiae vis ac soliditas consistit, cunctis fidelibus credendam et tenendam, secundum antiquam atque constantem universalis ecclesiae fidem, proponere, atque contrarios dominico gregi adeo perniciosos errores proscribere et condemnare."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p108","Chapter 1","We therefore teach and declare that, according to the testimony of the gospel, the primacy of jurisdiction over the whole church of God was immediately and directly promised and conferred upon the blessed apostle Peter by Christ the Lord.","Docemus itaque et declaramus, iuxta evangelii testimonia, primatum iurisdictionis in universam dei ecclesiam immediate et directe beato Petro apostolo promissum atque collatum a Christo domino fuisse."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p109","Chapter 1","For to Simon alone, to whom He had already said, \"You shall be called Cephas,\" after he professed, \"You are Christ, the Son of the living God,\" the Lord spoke with these solemn words: \"Blessed are you, Simon Bar-Jona, because flesh and blood has not revealed it to you, but My Father who is in heaven. And I say to you that you are Peter, and upon this rock I will build My church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it. And I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven; and whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.\"","Unum enim Simonem, cui iam pridem dixerat \"tu vocaberis Cephas,\" postquam ille suam edidit confessionem inquiens \"tu es Christus filius dei vivi,\" solemnibus his verbis allocutus est dominus: \"beatus es Simon Bar Iona, quia caro et sanguis non revelavit tibi, sed pater meus qui in caelis est. Et ego dico tibi, quia tu es Petrus, et super hanc petram aedificabo ecclesiam meam, et portae inferi non praevalebunt adversus eam. Et tibi dabo claves regni caelorum. Et quodcumque ligaveris super terram, erit ligatum et in caelis, et quodcumque solveris super terram, erit solutum et in caelis.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p110","Chapter 1","And to Simon Peter alone did Jesus, after His resurrection, confer the jurisdiction of the supreme shepherd and ruler over His whole flock, saying: \"Feed My lambs,\" \"feed My sheep.\"","Atque uni Simoni Petro contulit Iesus post suam resurrectionem summi pastoris et rectoris iurisdictionem in totum suum ovile, dicens: \"pasce agnos meos, pasce oves meas.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p111","Chapter 1","To this manifest doctrine of the sacred scriptures, as it has always been understood by the catholic church, the depraved opinions of those who, perverting the form of government established by Christ the Lord in His church, deny that Peter alone has been instructed by Christ with a true and proper primacy of jurisdiction over the other apostles, either separately or all together, or who affirm that the same primacy was not immediately and directly conferred upon the blessed Peter himself, but upon the church, and through it upon him as the minister of the church.","Huic tam manifestae sacrarum scripturarum doctrinae, ut ab ecclesia catholica semper intellecta est, aperte opponuntur pravae eorum sententiae qui, constitutam a Christo domino in sua ecclesia regiminis formam pervertentes, negant solum Petrum prae caeteris apostolis, sive seorsum singulis sive omnibus simul, vero proprio que iurisdictionis primatu fuisse a Christo instructum; aut qui affirmant eundem primatum non immediate directe que ipsi beato Petro, sed ecclesiae, et per hanc illi ut ipsius ecclesiae ministro, delatum fuisse."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p112","Chapter 1","If anyone therefore says that the blessed apostle Peter was not constituted by Christ the Lord as the prince of all the apostles and the visible head of the whole church militant, or that he received from the same Lord Jesus Christ only a primacy of honor, not a true and proper primacy of jurisdiction, let him be anathema.","Si quis igitur dixerit beatum Petrum apostolum non esse a Christo domino constitutum apostolorum omnium principem et totius ecclesiae militantis visibile caput; vel eundem honoris tantum, non autem verae propriae que iurisdictionis primatum ab eodem domino nostro Iesu Christo directe et immediate accepisse: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p113","Chapter 2","That which our Lord Jesus Christ, the Prince of Shepherds and the great Shepherd of the sheep, established in the blessed apostle Peter for the perpetual salvation and lasting good of the church, must of necessity, by the same author, perpetually remain in the church, which, founded upon the rock, will stand firm until the end of the ages.","Quod autem in beato apostolo Petro princeps pastorum et pastor magnus ovium dominus Christus Iesus in perpetuam salutem ac perenne bonum ecclesiae instituit, id eodem auctore in ecclesia, quae fundata super petram ad finem saeculorum usque firma stabit, iugiter durare necesse est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p114","Chapter 2","For no one doubts, indeed it is known to all ages, that the holy and most blessed Peter, prince and head of the apostles, the pillar of faith and the foundation of the catholic church, received the keys of the kingdom from our Lord Jesus Christ, the Savior and Redeemer of the human race; and that he lives and presides and exercises judgment to this time and always in his successors, the bishops of the holy Roman see, which was founded by him and consecrated by his blood.","Nulli sane dubium, imo saeculis omnibus notum est, quod sanctus beatissimus que Petrus, apostolorum princeps et caput, fidei que columna et ecclesiae catholicae fundamentum, a domino nostro Iesu Christo salvatore humani generis ac redemptore claves regni accepit; qui ad hoc usque tempus et semper in suis successoribus episcopis sanctae Romanae sedis, ab ipso fundatae eius que consecratae sanguine, vivit et praesidet et iudicium exercet."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p115","Chapter 2","Whence whoever succeeds Peter in this chair, he, according to the institution of Christ Himself, obtains the primacy of Peter over the universal church.","Unde quicumque in hac cathedra Petro succedit, is secundum Christi ipsius institutionem primatum Petri in universam ecclesiam obtinet."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p116","Chapter 2","\"The disposition of truth therefore remains, and blessed Peter, persevering in the strength of the rock which he received, has not abandoned the government of the church.\"","\"Manet ergo dispositio veritatis, et beatus Petrus, in accepta fortitudine petrae perseverans, suscepta ecclesiae gubernacula non reliquit.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p117","Chapter 2","For this reason, it has always been necessary for every church to resort to the Roman church, on account of its more powerful principality, that is, the faithful who are everywhere, that in that see, from which the rights of venerable communion emanate to all, they might, as members associated in the head, coalesce into one body.","Hac de causa ad Romanam ecclesiam propter potentiorem principalitatem necesse semper fuit omnem convenire ecclesiam, hoc est eos qui sunt undique fideles, ut in ea sede, e qua venerandae communionis iura in omnes dimanant, tamquam membra in capite consociata in unam corporis compagem coalescerent."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p118","Chapter 2","If anyone therefore says that it is not from the institution of Christ the Lord Himself (that is, by divine law) that blessed Peter has perpetual successors in the primacy over the universal church, or that the Roman pontiff is not the successor of blessed Peter in the same primacy: let him be anathema.","Si quis ergo dixerit non esse ex ipsius Christi domini institutione seu iure divino, ut beatus Petrus in primatu super universam ecclesiam habeat perpetuos successores, aut Romanum pontificem non esse beati Petri in eodem primatu successorem: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p119","Chapter 3","Therefore, leaning on the open testimonies of the sacred scriptures and adhering to the express and clear decrees both of our predecessors the Roman pontiffs and of the general councils, we renew the definition of the oecumenical council of Florence, by which it is to be believed by all the faithful of Christ that the holy apostolic see and the Roman pontiff holds the primacy over the whole world, and that the Roman pontiff himself is the successor of blessed Peter, the prince of the apostles, and the true vicar of Christ, and the head of the whole church, and the father and teacher of all Christians, and that to him in blessed Peter was given full power by our Lord Jesus Christ to feed, rule, and govern the universal church, as is also contained in the acts of the oecumenical councils and in the sacred canons.","Quapropter apertis innixi sacrarum litterarum testimoniis et inhaerentes tum praedecessorum nostrorum Romanorum pontificum tum conciliorum generalium disertis perspicuis que decretis, innovamus oecumenici concilii Florentini definitionem, qua credendum ab omnibus Christi fidelibus est sanctam apostolicam sedem et Romanum pontificem in universum orbem tenere primatum, et ipsum pontificem Romanum successorem esse beati Petri principis apostolorum, et verum Christi vicarium, totius que ecclesiae caput, et omnium christianorum patrem ac doctorem exsistere, et ipsi in beato Petro pascendi, regendi ac gubernandi universalem ecclesiam a domino nostro Iesu Christo plenam potestatem traditam esse, quemadmodum etiam in gestis oecumenicorum conciliorum et in sacris canonibus continetur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p120","Chapter 3","We therefore teach and declare that the Roman church, by the disposition of the Lord, holds the primacy of ordinary power over all others, and that this power of jurisdiction of the Roman pontiff, which is truly episcopal, is immediate, with respect to which the pastors and faithful of whatever rite and dignity, both separately and all together, are bound by the duty of hierarchical subordination and true obedience, not only in things which pertain to faith and morals, but also in those which pertain to the discipline and government of the church diffused throughout the whole world; so that, the unity of communion and of the profession of the same faith being preserved with the Roman pontiff, the church of Christ may be one flock under one supreme shepherd.","Docemus proinde et declaramus ecclesiam Romanam, disponente domino, super omnes alias ordinariae potestatis obtinere principatum, et hanc Romani pontificis iurisdictionis potestatem, quae vere episcopalis est, immediatam esse, erga quam cuiuscumque ritus et dignitatis pastores atque fideles, tam seorsum singuli quam simul omnes, officio hierarchicae subordinationis verae que obedientiae obstringuntur, non solum in rebus quae ad fidem et mores, sed etiam in iis quae ad disciplinam et regimen ecclesiae per totum orbem diffusae pertinent; ita ut, custodita cum Romano pontifice tam communionis quam eiusdem fidei professionis unitate, ecclesia Christi sit unus grex sub uno summo pastore."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p121","Chapter 3","This is the doctrine of catholic truth, from which no one can deviate without loss of faith and salvation.","Haec est catholicae veritatis doctrina, a qua deviare salva fide atque salute nemo potest."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p122","Chapter 3","So far, however, is this power of the supreme pontiff from hindering that ordinary and immediate power of episcopal jurisdiction by which bishops, who have succeeded in the place of the apostles as true pastors, feed and rule the flocks assigned to them each individually, that it is asserted, strengthened, and vindicated by the same supreme and universal shepherd, according to the word of St. Gregory the Great: \"My honor is the honor of the universal church. My honor is the solid vigor of my brothers. Then am I truly honored when due honor is not denied to each.\"","Tantum autem abest ut haec summi pontificis potestas officiat ordinariae ac immediatae illi episcopalis iurisdictionis potestati, qua episcopi, qui positi a spiritu sancto in apostolorum locum successerunt, tamquam veri pastores assignatos sibi greges singuli singulos pascunt et regunt, ut eadem a supremo et universali pastore asseratur, roboretur ac vindicetur, secundum illud sancti Gregorii magni: \"meus honor est honor universalis ecclesiae. Meus honor est fratrum meorum solidus vigor. Tum ego vere honoratus sum, cum singulis quibusque honor debitus non negatur.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p123","Chapter 3","Moreover, from that supreme power of the Roman pontiff to govern the universal church, it follows that he has the right to communicate freely with the pastors and flocks of the whole church in the exercise of this duty, that they may be taught and ruled by him in the way of salvation.","Porro ex suprema illa Romani pontificis potestate gubernandi universam ecclesiam, ius eidem esse consequitur in huius sui muneris exercitio libere communicandi cum pastoribus et gregibus totius ecclesiae, ut iidem ab ipso in via salutis doceri ac regi possint."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p124","Chapter 3","Wherefore we condemn and reprove the opinions of those who say that this communication of the supreme head with the pastors and flocks can be lawfully hindered, or that it is subject to the secular power, so that they contend that those things which are established by the apostolic see or by its authority for the government of the church have no force or value unless confirmed by the pleasure of the secular power.","Quare damnamus ac reprobamus illorum sententias qui hanc supremi capitis cum pastoribus et gregibus communicationem licite impediri posse dicunt, aut eandem reddunt saeculari potestati obnoxiam, ita ut contendant quae ab apostolica sede vel eius auctoritate ad regimen ecclesiae constituuntur vim ac valorem non habere nisi potestatis saecularis placito confirmentur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p125","Chapter 3","And since by divine right of apostolic primacy the Roman pontiff presides over the universal church, we also teach and declare that he is the supreme judge of the faithful, and that in all causes pertaining to ecclesiastical examination recourse can be had to his judgment, and that the judgment of the apostolic see, whose authority is not surpassed, is to be reviewed by no one, nor is it lawful for anyone to judge of his judgment.","Et quoniam divino apostolici primatus iure Romanus pontifex universae ecclesiae praeest, docemus etiam et declaramus eum esse iudicem supremum fidelium, et in omnibus causis ad examen ecclesiasticum spectantibus ad ipsius posse iudicium recurri; sedis vero apostolicae, cuius auctoritate maior non est, iudicium a nemine fore retractandum, neque cuiquam de eius licere iudicare iudicio."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p126","Chapter 3","Wherefore they wander from the straight path of truth who affirm that it is lawful to appeal from the judgments of the Roman pontiffs to an oecumenical council, as to an authority superior to the Roman pontiff.","Quare a recto veritatis tramite aberrant qui affirmant licere ab iudiciis Romanorum pontificum ad oecumenicum concilium tamquam ad auctoritatem Romano pontifice superiorem appellare."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p127","Chapter 3","If anyone therefore says that the Roman pontiff has only the office of inspection or direction, but not the full and supreme power of jurisdiction over the universal church, not only in things which pertain to faith and morals, but also in those which pertain to the discipline and government of the church diffused throughout the whole world; or that he has only the principal parts, but not the whole plenitude of this supreme power; or that this power of his is not ordinary and immediate over all and each of the churches, and over all and each of the pastors and faithful: let him be anathema.","Si quis itaque dixerit Romanum pontificem habere tantummodo officium inspectionis vel directionis, non autem plenam et supremam potestatem iurisdictionis in universam ecclesiam, non solum in rebus quae ad fidem et mores, sed etiam in iis quae ad disciplinam et regimen ecclesiae per totum orbem diffusae pertinent; aut eum habere tantum potiores partes, non vero totam plenitudinem huius supremae potestatis; aut hanc eius potestatem non esse ordinariam et immediatam, sive in omnes ac singulas ecclesias, sive in omnes et singulos pastores et fideles: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p128","Chapter 4","But the apostolic primacy which the Roman pontiff holds as the successor of Peter the prince of the apostles over the universal church includes also the supreme power of magisterium. This holy see has always held, the perpetual use of the church confirms, and the oecumenical councils themselves declare, especially those in which the east and the west met in union of faith and charity, that this supreme power of magisterium is comprehended in the apostolic primacy.","Ipso autem apostolico primatu, quem Romanus pontifex tamquam Petri principis apostolorum successor in universam ecclesiam obtinet, supremam quoque magisterii potestatem comprehendi, haec sancta sedes semper tenuit, perpetuus ecclesiae usus comprobat, ipsa que oecumenica concilia, ea imprimis in quibus oriens cum occidente in fidei charitatis que unionem conveniebat, declaraverunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p129","Chapter 4","For the fathers of the fourth council of Constantinople, following in the footsteps of their predecessors, put forth this solemn profession: \"The first condition of salvation is to keep the rule of the true faith. And because the sentence of our Lord Jesus Christ cannot be omitted, who said, 'You are Peter, and upon this rock I will build My church,' these things which have been said are proved by events, because in the apostolic see the catholic religion has always been kept immaculate, and holy doctrine has been celebrated.\"","Patres enim concilii constantinopolitani quarti, maiorum vestigiis inhaerentes, hanc solemnem ediderunt professionem: \"prima salus est rectae fidei regulam custodire. Et quia non potest domini nostri Iesu Christi praetermitti sententia dicentis 'tu es Petrus et super hanc petram aedificabo ecclesiam meam,' haec quae dicta sunt rerum probantur effectibus, quia in sede apostolica immaculata est semper catholica reservata religio et sancta celebrata doctrina.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p130","Chapter 4","And the Greeks, with the approval of the second council of Lyons, professed: \"The holy Roman church holds the supreme and full primacy and principality over the whole catholic church, which it truly and humbly recognizes that it has received from the Lord Himself in blessed Peter, the prince of the apostles, whose successor the Roman pontiff is, with the fullness of power; and as it is bound before all others to defend the truth of faith, so, if any questions concerning faith shall arise, they ought to be defined by its judgment.\"","Approbante vero Lugdunensi concilio secundo, Graeci professi sunt: \"sanctam Romanam ecclesiam summum et plenum primatum et principatum super universam ecclesiam catholicam obtinere, quem se ab ipso domino in beato Petro apostolorum principe sive vertice, cuius Romanus pontifex est successor, cum potestatis plenitudine recepisse veraciter et humiliter recognoscit; et sicut prae caeteris tenetur fidei veritatem defendere, sic et si quae de fide subortae fuerint quaestiones, suo debent iudicio definiri.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p131","Chapter 4","Finally, the council of Florence defined: \"The Roman pontiff is the true vicar of Christ, the head of the whole church, and the father and teacher of all Christians, and to him in blessed Peter was given full power by our Lord Jesus Christ to feed, rule, and govern the universal church.\"","Florentinum denique concilium definivit: \"pontificem Romanum verum Christi vicarium, totius que ecclesiae caput et omnium christianorum patrem et doctorem exsistere, et ipsi in beato Petro pascendi, regendi ac gubernandi universalem ecclesiam a domino nostro Iesu Christo plenam potestatem traditam esse.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p132","Chapter 4","To satisfy this pastoral duty, our predecessors have given unceasing effort that the salutary doctrine of Christ might be propagated among all the peoples of the earth, and with equal care they watched that, where it was received, it might be preserved sincere and pure.","Huic pastorali muneri ut satisfacerent, praedecessores nostri indefessam semper operam dederunt, ut salutaris Christi doctrina apud omnes terrae populos propagaretur, pari que cura vigilarunt, ut ubi recepta esset, sincera et pura conservaretur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p133","Chapter 4","Wherefore the prelates of the whole world, sometimes singly, sometimes gathered in synods, following the long custom of the churches and the form of the ancient rule, referred especially those perils which arose in matters of faith to this apostolic see, that there especially might be repaired the damages of the faith where the faith cannot suffer defect.","Quocirca totius orbis antistites, nunc singuli, nunc in synodis congregati, longam ecclesiarum consuetudinem et antiquae regulae formam sequentes, ea praesertim pericula quae in negotiis fidei emergebant ad hanc sedem apostolicam retulerunt, ut ibi potissimum resarcirentur damna fidei, ubi fides non potest sentire defectum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p134","Chapter 4","But the Roman pontiffs, as the condition of times and affairs suggested, sometimes by calling oecumenical councils, sometimes by seeking the opinion of the church scattered through the world, sometimes by particular synods, sometimes by other helps which divine providence supplied, defined those things to be held which by God's help they knew to be consonant with the sacred scriptures and the apostolic traditions.","Romani autem pontifices, prout temporum et rerum conditio suadebat, nunc convocatis oecumenicis conciliis, aut explorata ecclesiae per orbem dispersae sententia, nunc per synodos particulares, nunc aliis quae divina suppeditabat providentia adhibitis auxiliis, ea tenenda definiverunt quae sacris scripturis et apostolicis traditionibus consentanea, deo adiutore, cognoverant."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p135","Chapter 4","For the Holy Spirit was not promised to the successors of Peter that by His revelation they might disclose new doctrine, but that, by His assistance, they might holily guard and faithfully set forth the revelation handed down by the apostles, that is, the deposit of faith.","Neque enim Petri successoribus spiritus sanctus promissus est, ut eo revelante novam doctrinam patefacerent, sed ut eo assistente traditam per apostolos revelationem seu fidei depositum sancte custodirent et fideliter exponerent."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p136","Chapter 4","Whose apostolic doctrine all the venerable fathers embraced and the holy orthodox doctors venerated and followed, knowing most fully that this see of holy Peter remains always untainted by any error, according to the divine promise of the Lord our Savior made to the prince of His disciples: \"I have prayed for you, that your faith not fail; and when you have turned again, strengthen your brothers.\"","Quorum quidem apostolicam doctrinam omnes venerabiles patres amplexi et sancti doctores orthodoxi venerati atque secuti sunt, plenissime scientes hanc sancti Petri sedem ab omni semper errore illibatam permanere, secundum domini salvatoris nostri divinam pollicitationem discipulorum suorum principi factam: \"ego rogavi pro te, ut non deficiat fides tua; et tu aliquando conversus, confirma fratres tuos.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p137","Chapter 4","This gift of truth and of never-failing faith was therefore divinely conferred upon Peter and his successors in this chair that they might exercise their lofty office for the salvation of all; that the whole flock of Christ, turned away by them from the poisonous food of error, might be nourished with the pasture of heavenly doctrine; that, the occasion of schism being removed, the whole church might be preserved one, and, resting on its foundation, might stand firm against the gates of hell.","Hoc igitur veritatis et fidei nunquam deficientis charisma Petro eius que in hac cathedra successoribus divinitus collatum est, ut excelso suo munere in omnium salutem fungerentur; ut universus Christi grex, per eos ab erroris venenosa esca aversus, caelestis doctrinae pabulo nutriretur; ut sublata schismatis occasione, ecclesia tota una conservaretur atque suo fundamento innixa firma adversus inferi portas consisteret."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p138","Chapter 4","But since in this very age in which the saving efficacy of the apostolic office is especially required, not a few are found who detract from its authority, we deem it altogether necessary solemnly to assert the prerogative which the only-begotten Son of God deigned to join with the supreme pastoral office.","At vero cum ipsa aetate qua salutifera apostolici muneris efficacia vel maxime requiritur, non pauci inveniantur qui illius auctoritati obtrectant, necessarium omnino esse censemus praerogativam quam unigenitus dei filius cum summo pastorali officio coniungere dignatus est solemniter asserere."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p139","Chapter 4","Therefore, faithfully adhering to the tradition received from the beginning of the Christian faith, for the glory of God our Savior, the exaltation of the catholic religion, and the salvation of Christian peoples, with the sacred council approving, we teach and define as a divinely revealed dogma: that the Roman pontiff, when he speaks ex cathedra, that is, when, exercising the office of pastor and teacher of all Christians, he defines by his supreme apostolic authority a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the universal church, by the divine assistance promised to him in blessed Peter, possesses that infallibility with which the divine Redeemer willed that His church should be endowed in defining doctrine concerning faith or morals; and therefore that such definitions of the Roman pontiff are irreformable of themselves, and not from the consent of the church.","Itaque nos traditioni a fidei christianae exordio perceptae fideliter inhaerendo, ad dei salvatoris nostri gloriam, religionis catholicae exaltationem et christianorum populorum salutem, sacro approbante concilio, docemus et divinitus revelatum dogma esse definimus: Romanum pontificem, cum ex cathedra loquitur, id est, cum omnium christianorum pastoris et doctoris munere fungens, pro suprema sua apostolica auctoritate doctrinam de fide vel moribus ab universa ecclesia tenendam definit, per assistentiam divinam ipsi in beato Petro promissam, ea infallibilitate pollere, qua divinus redemptor ecclesiam suam in definienda doctrina de fide vel moribus instructam esse voluit; ideo que eiusmodi Romani pontificis definitiones ex sese, non autem ex consensu ecclesiae, irreformabiles esse."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p140","Chapter 4","But if anyone (which may God forbid) presumes to contradict this our definition: let him be anathema.","Si quis autem huic nostrae definitioni contradicere (quod deus avertat) praesumpserit: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p141","Canons of the Dogmatic Constitution Pastor Aeternus (session 4 — 18 July 1870)","If anyone says that blessed Peter the apostle was not constituted by Christ the Lord as the prince of all the apostles and the visible head of the whole church militant, or that he received from the same Lord Jesus Christ only a primacy of honor, not a true and proper primacy of jurisdiction: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit beatum Petrum apostolum non esse a Christo domino constitutum apostolorum omnium principem et totius ecclesiae militantis visibile caput; vel eundem honoris tantum, non autem verae propriae que iurisdictionis primatum ab eodem domino nostro Iesu Christo directe et immediate accepisse: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p142","Canon 2","If anyone says that it is not from the institution of Christ the Lord Himself (that is, by divine law) that blessed Peter has perpetual successors in the primacy over the universal church, or that the Roman pontiff is not the successor of blessed Peter in the same primacy: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit non esse ex ipsius Christi domini institutione seu iure divino, ut beatus Petrus in primatu super universam ecclesiam habeat perpetuos successores, aut Romanum pontificem non esse beati Petri in eodem primatu successorem: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p143","Canon 3","If anyone says that the Roman pontiff has only the office of inspection or direction, but not the full and supreme power of jurisdiction over the universal church, not only in things which pertain to faith and morals, but also in those which pertain to the discipline and government of the church diffused throughout the whole world; or that he has only the principal parts, but not the whole plenitude of this supreme power; or that this power of his is not ordinary and immediate over all and each of the churches, and over all and each of the pastors and faithful: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit Romanum pontificem habere tantummodo officium inspectionis vel directionis, non autem plenam et supremam potestatem iurisdictionis in universam ecclesiam, non solum in rebus quae ad fidem et mores, sed etiam in iis quae ad disciplinam et regimen ecclesiae per totum orbem diffusae pertinent; aut eum habere tantum potiores partes, non vero totam plenitudinem huius supremae potestatis; aut hanc eius potestatem non esse ordinariam et immediatam, sive in omnes ac singulas ecclesias, sive in omnes et singulos pastores et fideles: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p144","Canon 4","If anyone says that the Roman pontiff, when he speaks ex cathedra, that is, when exercising the office of pastor and teacher of all Christians, by his supreme apostolic authority defines a doctrine concerning faith or morals to be held by the universal church, does not possess that infallibility with which the divine Redeemer willed that His church should be endowed in defining doctrine concerning faith or morals; or that such definitions of the Roman pontiff are not irreformable of themselves, but only with the consent of the church: let him be anathema.","Si quis dixerit Romanum pontificem, cum ex cathedra loquitur, id est, cum omnium christianorum pastoris et doctoris munere fungens, pro suprema sua apostolica auctoritate doctrinam de fide vel moribus ab universa ecclesia tenendam definit, ea infallibilitate non pollere, qua divinus redemptor ecclesiam suam in definienda doctrina de fide vel moribus instructam esse voluit; aut eiusmodi Romani pontificis definitiones ex sese, non autem ex consensu ecclesiae, irreformabiles non esse: anathema sit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p145","First Vatican Council — Prorogation Decree","The sacred oecumenical Vatican council, legitimately assembled in the Holy Spirit, decrees that the session appointed for the discussion and definition of the remaining matters concerning the church and other topics is to be deferred, and that the council itself is to be prorogued until a more opportune time, whenever it shall seem expedient to the supreme pontiff.","Sacrosanctum oecumenicum Vaticanum concilium, in spiritu sancto legitime congregatum, decernit sessionem pro reliquis de ecclesia aliis que argumentis tractandis et definiendis indictam prorogandam esse, ipsum que concilium ad opportunius tempus, quoties summo pontifici expedire visum fuerit, prorogari."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p146","First Vatican Council — Prorogation Decree","Given at Rome, at St. Peter's, in the public session solemnly held on the 18th day of July, 1870.","Datum Romae, apud sanctum Petrum, in publica sessione solemniter habita die decima octava mensis iulii anno MDCCCLXX."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p147","First Vatican Council — Prorogation Decree","DISCLAIMER: The following text is a draft schema prepared for the First Vatican Council but never promulgated as a conciliar decree. It was submitted to the fathers for examination but was not voted upon or approved before the council was prorogued. Therefore, it does not possess conciliar authority and is presented here only for historical completeness.","MONITUM: Textus sequens est schema pro Concilio Vaticano I confectum sed numquam ut decretum conciliare promulgatum. Patribus ad examen propositum fuit sed ante concilii prorogationem nec suffragatum nec approbatum est. Quare auctoritatem conciliarem non habet et hic tantum ad integritatem historicam exhibetur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p148","Schema on Catholic Doctrine","Pius, bishop, servant of the servants of God, for an everlasting record, with the sacred council approving.","Pius episcopus servus servorum dei ad perpetuam rei memoriam sacro approbante concilio."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p149","Schema on Catholic Doctrine","The solicitude of the apostolic office, that the word of eternal truth and salvation may be kept undefiled and that pernicious errors may be driven away from the Christian people, urges us now the more urgently the more grave perils threaten the Lord's flock from all sides from the perversity of opinions freely raging.","Apostolici muneris sollicitudo, ut verbum aeternae veritatis et salutis illibatum conservetur ac perniciosi errores a populo christiano depellantur, eo nunc instantius nos urget quo ex libere grassantium opinionum perversitate graviora undique dominico gregi imminent pericula."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p150","Schema on Catholic Doctrine","For many antichrists have gone out at this time who wholly assail divine revelation and strive to subvert all the foundations of all religion, and, as far as in them lies, openly endeavor to bring back the human race from the light of Christian faith to the night of blind infidelity.","Exierunt enim hoc tempore antichristi multi, qui universam oppugnant revelationem divinam, ipsa que omnis religionis fundamenta subvertere ac genus humanum quantum in ipsis est a christianae fidei luce ad noctem caecae infidelitatis reducere aperte conantur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p151","Schema on Catholic Doctrine","Others, however, led away by various and strange doctrines, miserably mixing nature and grace, human knowledge and divine faith, are known to deprave the true sense of the dogmas which the church holds and teaches, and to bring the integrity and sincerity of the faith into peril.","Alii vero doctrinis variis et peregrinis abducti, naturam et gratiam, scientiam humanam et fidem divinam misere commiscentes, verum sensum dogmatum quem tenet et docet ecclesia depravare et integritatem sinceritatem que fidei in periculum adducere noscuntur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p152","Schema on Catholic Doctrine","Wherefore it has seemed good to us in the Holy Spirit both to fortify all the faithful against that open impiety, and, against the more stealthily creeping errors, to set forth and declare catholic doctrine for the edification of the body of Christ, in which we have been taught, as truth is in Jesus.","Quare visum est nobis in spiritu sancto tum omnes fideles adversus apertam illam impietatem praemunire, tum contra occultius serpentes errores catholicam doctrinam proponere ac declarare in aedificationem corporis Christi, in quo edocti sumus sicut est veritas in Iesu."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p153","Concerning Materialism and Pantheism","For who would not groan that among the Christian nations themselves men who have become vain in their thoughts have come into this depth of darkness and baseness that they affirm (and, which is more horrible, are permitted to teach publicly and with impunity) that besides matter and the transformations or explications of matter nothing real exists; or that there is only one essence both of God and of all things which are, and that this essence by its necessary and continuous manifestation or explication or emanation becomes all things, or from it all things which exist come into being, so that in the very explication of the essence God Himself becomes the world, or the world becomes God.","Quis enim vero non ingemiscat inter ipsas nationes christianas homines qui evanuerunt in cogitationibus suis in hoc tenebrarum ac turpitudinis profundum devenisse, ut affirment (et quod horrendum magis publice atque impune docere permittantur) praeter materiam materiae que transformationes aut explicationes nihil reale exsistere; aut unicam esse essentiam tum dei tum rerum omnium quae sunt, hanc que essentiam necessaria continua que sui manifestatione aut explicatione aut emanatione fieri omnia, vel ex ea fieri omnia quae existunt, ita ut in eadem essentiae explicatione ipse deus fiat mundus, aut mundus fiat deus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p154","Concerning Materialism and Pantheism","To their impiety in the sentiments themselves they add another in words, that they are accustomed to cover the monsters of opinions which they have contrived with the most holy names of the mysteries of the Christian religion, and while they name the Trinity, incarnation, redemption, resurrection, they seek so to persuade the incautious that something Christian underlies their doctrines, and to twist and deprave the venerable mysteries of true religion to the most perverse senses.","Suae autem impietati in sententiis ipsis supperaddunt aliam in verbis, quod monstra opinionum quae confinxerunt sanctissimis nominibus mysteriorum christianae religionis obtegere consueverunt, atque dum trinitatem, incarnationem, redemptionem, resurrectionem nominant, id assequi student ut suis doctrinis aliquid christianum subesse incautis persuadeant, ac ipsa veneranda mysteria verae religionis ad perversissimos sensus detorqueant atque depravent."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p155","Concerning Materialism and Pantheism","Detesting the impious folly in all forms of materialism and pantheism, we recall the miserable to the elements of faith and of our symbol by which we believe and profess that there is one God, the creator of heaven and earth, of all things visible and invisible, who by His almighty power and most free counsel simultaneously from the beginning of time founded both creations from nothing, the spiritual and the corporal, namely angelic and worldly, and then human, as it were common, constituted from spirit and body.","Impiam insipientiam in omnibus materialismi et pantheismi formis detestantes, revocamus miseros ad elementa fidei ac symboli nostri quo credimus ac profitemur unum esse deum creatorem caeli et terrae, visibilium omnium et invisibilium, qui sua omnipotente virtute et liberrimo consilio simul ab initio temporis utramque de nihilo condidit creaturam spiritualem et corporalem, angelicam videlicet et mundanam, ac deinde humanam quasi communem ex spiritu et corpore constitutam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p156","Concerning God and the Natural Knowledge of Him","God, therefore, since He is one, singular, altogether simple and unchangeable essence, eternal and necessarily existing, infinite in intellect and will and in every perfection, is to be professed infinitely exalted above all things which exist besides Him or can be conceived.","Deus igitur, cum sit una singularis simplex omnino et incommutabilis essentia, aeternus et necessario existens, intellectu ac voluntate omni que perfectione infinitus, profitendus est super omnia quae praeter ipsum sunt aut concipi possunt infinite exaltatus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p157","Concerning God and the Natural Knowledge of Him","Nor is it to be doubted that the true God can be known by the natural light of human reason itself through those things which are made, since the Holy Spirit testifies in the book of Wisdom that \"by the greatness of the beauty and of the creature, the creator of them may be seen so as to be known thereby,\" and the apostle Paul teaches that the gentiles are inexcusable because, when they had known God from this natural manifestation through creatures, they did not glorify Him as God or give thanks, because \"that which is known of God is manifest in them, for God has manifested it to them. For the invisible things of Him from the creation of the world are clearly seen, being understood by the things that are made.\"","Neque dubitandum est verum deum naturali ipsius humanae rationis lumine per ea quae facta sunt posse cognosci, quandoquidem testante spiritu sancto in libro sapientiae \"a magnitudine speciei et creaturae cognoscibiliter poterit creator horum videri,\" et Paulus apostolus docet gentiles in eo esse inexcusabiles quod cum ex hac naturali manifestatione per creaturas deum cognovissent, non sicut deum glorificaverunt aut gratias egerunt, quia \"quod notum est dei manifestum est in illis; deus enim illis manifestavit. Invisibilia enim ipsius a creatura mundi per ea quae facta sunt intellecta conspiciuntur.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p158","Concerning God and the Natural Knowledge of Him","But created reason, understanding itself also wholly to depend upon God as its beginning and end, cannot but acknowledge that it is so subject to God, who is truth itself and the fountain of all truth, that if it has pleased His infinite wisdom and goodness to reveal Himself to men in another way than through those things which are made and by another way than by the natural light of reason itself, they ought to render the full obedience of intellect and will.","At ratio creata, intelligens pariter se ipsam totam dependere a deo ut a suo principio et fine, nequit non agnoscere deo, qui est ipsa veritas omnis que veritatis fons, ita se esse subiectam, ut si infinitae sapientiae ac bonitati placuerit alio modo quam per ea quae facta sunt et alio quam ipsius rationis naturali lumine sese hominibus revelare, plenum iidem debeant praestare intellectus ac voluntatis obsequium."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p159","Concerning God and the Natural Knowledge of Him","But just as God can raise man to know truths and to a mode of knowing which the natural light of reason does not reach, so He can exalt him to such a participation of divine goods and to such a fellowship and society with God Himself as altogether surpass the natural powers and perfections of the creature.","Sicut autem potest deus hominem evehere ad veritates cognoscendas et ad modum cognoscendi quo naturale rationis lumen non pertingit, ita potest eum exaltare ad eiusmodi participationem bonorum divinorum et ad eiusmodi consortium ac societatem cum ipso deo quae naturales creaturae vires et perfectiones omnino superent."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p160","Concerning God and the Natural Knowledge of Him","Moreover, this supernatural revelation and communication of God to men, since it is not only possible but most certainly exists, in which the whole divine religion consists, hence surely that profane speech creeping like a cancer is to be an abomination to all Christians, of those men who, changing the truth of God into a lie in the midst of the light of the Christian religion, say that human reason is altogether independent and that in itself it is the fountain and sole norm and law of all truth and all goodness, so that above natural cognitions of reason no truth is to be believed even if revealed by divine works and authority, and faith cannot be commanded, nor can man be raised by God Himself beyond the unfolding of his rational nature and natural powers to a higher communication and to a higher good, but that reason and human nature can and ought of themselves by continual progress to attain to all final truth and goodness.","Haec porro supernaturalis revelatio et communicatio dei ad homines, cum non solum possibilis sed factum sit certissimum, in quo religio divina tota constituitur, hinc profecto christianorum omnium abominationi exponendus est profanus sermo ut cancer serpens illorum hominum qui, media in luce religionis christianae commutantes veritatem dei in mendacium, dicunt humanam rationem esse penitus independentem atque in se ipsa esse omnis veri omnis que boni fontem et unicam normam ac legem, ita ut supra naturales rationis cognitiones veritas credenda ne divina quidem opera et auctoritate revelari ac fides imperari queat, neque ab ipso deo homo ultra naturae suae rationalis naturalium que virium explicationem ad altiorem communicationem et ad bonum altius evehi possit, sed rationem naturam que humanam iugi profectu omnem tandem veri et boni perfectionem ex sese assequi posse ac debere."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p161","Concerning the Supernatural Order and Divine Revelation","Therefore, execrating such monstrous errors and at the same time, from the duty of our supreme pastoral office and from the feeling of mind by which we are most solicitous for the eternal salvation of souls, we admonish and adjure especially those who preside and those who exercise the function of educating and instructing youth, whether privately or publicly in schools, to keep this pestilence of spreading incredulity from the minds of their subjects and to fortify them against the danger of falling into it.","Huiusmodi igitur errorum portenta exsecrantes, simul ex supremi pastoralis officii nostri debito et animi sensu quo maxime possumus pro aeterna animarum salute sollicito, admonemus et obtestamur eos potissimum qui praesunt et qui privatim vel publice in scholis educandae et erudiendae iuventutis munere funguntur, ut ab animis subditorum hanc pestem grassantis incredulitatis arceant illos que contra pericula in eam incidendi praemuniant."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p162","Concerning the Supernatural Order and Divine Revelation","But truly it is not enough to have shuddered at those monsters of impiety, which are easily understood to be avoided by all who wish to be Christians, but since, as by a certain derivation from that falsely asserted independence of reason, the word of truth is mishandled by some who, erring concerning the nature of faith and of Christian knowledge and concerning the relationship of human science to divine faith, and leading others into error, have at this time taught things which are not fitting, therefore against the circumventions of such errors we intend to set forth and declare the catholic doctrine to be firmly retained by all.","Verum satis profecto non est illa monstra impietatis exhorruisse, quae ab omnibus qui christiani esse velint per se facile intelliguntur devitanda; sed quoniam velut derivatione quadam ab illa falso asserta independentia rationis verbum veritatis a quibusdam non recte tractatur, qui circa ipsam naturam fidei et cognitionis christianae et circa habitudinem scientiae humanae ad fidem divinam errantes et in errorem mittentes hoc tempore docuerunt quae non oportet, idcirco contra huiusmodi errorum circumventiones catholicam doctrinam firmiter ab omnibus retinendam exponere et declarare intendimus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p163","Concerning the Nature of Faith and Its Relation to Reason","First, according to the faith of the universal church and the declaration of the sacred Tridentine council, we profess and teach that the whole catholic revelation is contained in the written books both of the old and the new testament and in the unwritten traditions which, received from the mouth of Christ Himself by the apostles or handed down by the apostles themselves, the Holy Spirit dictating, have come down to us as it were by hand.","Imprimis secundum fidem universalis ecclesiae et sacri Tridentini concilii declarationem, profitemur et docemus integram catholicam revelationem contineri in libris scriptis tam veteris quam novi testamenti et sine scripto traditionibus, quae ex ipsius Christi ore ab apostolis acceptae, aut ab ipsis apostolis spiritu sancto dictante, quasi per manus traditae ad nos usque pervenerunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p164","Concerning the Nature of Faith and Its Relation to Reason","Since, however, various errors are scattered in our age concerning the books of sacred scripture themselves, we again declare and define that all those books, with all their parts, as contained in the decree of the oecumenical council of Trent, are to be received as sacred and canonical.","Quoniam vero de ipsis sacrae scripturae libris varii nostra aetate sparguntur errores, iterum declaramus et definimus eosdem libros omnes cum omnibus suis partibus, quemadmodum in decreto oecumenici concilii Tridentini continetur, pro sacris et canonicis suscipiendos esse."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p165","Concerning the Nature of Faith and Its Relation to Reason","But sacred and canonical they are to be believed, not because they were written by human industry only and then by the authority of the church were placed into the canon of the sacred scriptures, nor because they contain divine revelation without error, but because they were written by the inspiration of the Holy Spirit, and therefore are divinely inspired scriptures which have God as their author, and thus contain truly and properly the written word of God.","Sacri autem et canonici credendi sunt, non quod humana tantum ope scripti, auctoritate tamen ecclesiae in canonem sacrarum scripturarum relati sint, neque propterea solum quod divinam revelationem sine errore contineant, sed eo quod spiritu sancto inspirante conscripti fuerunt, ideo que sunt scripturae divinitus inspiratae, quae habent auctorem deum, atque ita continent vere et proprie verbum dei scriptum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p166","Concerning the Nature of Faith and Its Relation to Reason","Wherefore we declare and condemn as heretical the opinion of anyone who denies that any book, in whole or in part, of those which the Tridentine synod defined as to be received as sacred and canonical with all their parts, as they are accustomed to be read in the catholic church and as they are contained in the old Vulgate Latin edition, is divinely inspired.","Quare haereticam esse declaramus et damnamus sententiam, si quis divinitus inspiratum esse negaverit aliquem vel integrum vel ex parte librum de his quos Tridentina synodus integros cum omnibus suis partibus, prout in ecclesia catholica legi consueverunt et in veteri vulgata Latina editione habentur, pro sacris et canonicis suscipiendos esse definivit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p167","Concerning the Interpretation of Sacred Scripture","And because there are not wanting petulant minds by which sacred scripture is twisted to alien senses contrary to that sense which holy mother church has held and holds, therefore, renewing what was prescribed by the sacred Tridentine council concerning the interpretation of scripture, we declare that in matters of faith and morals pertaining to the edification of Christian doctrine, that sense of sacred scripture is to be held as true which it shall be established has been declared or defined by the church, the infallible guardian and interpreter of the word of God, or by the unanimous consent of the fathers.","Et quia non desunt petulantia ingenia a quibus scriptura sacra ad alienos sensus detorquetur contra eum sensum quem tenuit et tenet sancta mater ecclesia, hinc innovantes quae a sacra Tridentina synodo circa scripturarum interpretationem praescripta fuerunt, declaramus in rebus fidei et morum ad aedificationem doctrinae christianae pertinentium illum sacrae scripturae sensum verum habendum esse quem ab ecclesia, infallibili verbi dei custode ac interprete, vel unanimi consensione patrum declaratum aut definitum esse constiterit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p168","Concerning the Necessity of Supernatural Revelation","Although God, even through those things which are made and the light of human reason, manifesting Himself, is to be glorified according to the admonition of the apostle, and therefore thanks are to be rendered to Him, yet He wished, out of His infinite goodness, wisdom, and mercy, for the eternal salvation of men, to reveal Himself and His unsearchable riches and the dispensation of the mystery hidden from the ages by another supernatural manifestation, which is made to us through the word of God, not as by a demonstration for seeing, but by a proposition through the authority of God for believing.","Quamvis deus etiam per ea quae facta sunt humanae que rationis lumine sese manifestans, iuxta monitum apostoli, glorificandus et propterea ei gratiae agendae sint, voluit tamen ipse pro sua infinita bonitate, sapientia et misericordia ad aeternam hominum salutem se ipsum investigabiles que divitias suas ac dispensationem sacramenti a saeculis absconditi revelare manifestatione alia supernaturali, quae fit nobis per verbum dei, non quasi demonstratione ad videndum, sed propositione per auctoritatem dei ad credendum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p169","Concerning the Necessity of Supernatural Revelation","For \"God, who at sundry times and in divers manners spoke in times past to the fathers by the prophets, last of all in these days has spoken to us by His Son.\"","\"Multifariam enim multis que modis olim deus loquens patribus in prophetis, novissime locutus est nobis in filio.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p170","Concerning the Necessity of Supernatural Revelation","This supernatural revelation we declare to be necessary for men, not only as a help to more easily and universally obtain those things which are already contained in natural manifestation and could be understood from it, but its necessity for men is absolute according to the merciful plan of the divine dispensation, because the infinite goodness of God willed to raise the human race to a supernatural order and in this order to the knowledge of supernatural things through faith.","Hanc vero supernaturalem revelationem declaramus necessariam esse hominibus, non solum velut subsidium ad facilius et universalius assequenda ea quae in manifestatione naturali iam continerentur et ex ea intelligi possent, sed eius necessitatem pro hominibus esse omnimodam secundum misericors consilium divinae dispensationis, eo quod infinita dei bonitas humanum genus voluit elevare ad supernaturalem ordinem et in hoc ordine ad supernaturalium rerum cognitionem per fidem."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p171","Concerning the Necessity of Supernatural Revelation","For besides that natural knowledge alone would not correspond to the supernatural end, those dogmas, since they are supernatural, are not contained in the natural manifestation of God, and therefore human reason by its own light could never attain them.","Praeterquam quod enim naturalis tantummodo cognitio fini supernaturali non responderet, dogmata illa cum sint supernaturalia in naturali dei manifestatione non continentur, atque ideo humana ratio suo lumine ea assequi nunquam potuisset."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p172","Concerning Mysteries","Moreover, to restrain the rashness of certain men of our age who wish to be wiser than they ought to be and not to be sober, adhering to the perpetual sense and constant doctrine of the church, we teach and declare that among the truths divinely revealed there are properly so-called mysteries, which, although they are not contrary to reason, are nevertheless above reason.","Porro ad temeritatem quorundam hominum nostrae aetatis cohibendam, qui voluerunt plus sapere quam oportet sapere et non ad sobrietatem, perpetuo ecclesiae sensui et constanti doctrinae inhaerentes, docemus et declaramus inter veritates divinitus revelatas esse mysteria proprie dicta, quae licet non sint contra rationem, sunt tamen supra rationem."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p173","Concerning Mysteries","For although, faith leading, some understanding of them is attained, yet this knowledge itself is based on revealed principles; never, however, can human reason, however cultivated, become capable of attaining by its own powers and demonstrating from its own principles the existence and truth of such mysteries.","Quamvis enim fide praelucente ad eorum aliquam intelligentiam perveniatur, haec tamen ipsa cognitio principiis innititur revelatis; nunquam vero ratio humana, quantumvis exculta, effici potest idonea ad existentiam et veritatem mysteriorum huiusmodi suis viribus assequendam atque ex suis principiis demonstrandam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p174","Concerning Mysteries","For the apostle Paul teaches that besides that which is known of God and manifest even through those things which are made, there are \"the deep things of God\" which only the Spirit of God searches, but God Himself has revealed them to us through His Spirit, that we may believe by faith.","Paulus sane apostolus, praeter id quod notum est dei et manifestum etiam per ea quae facta sunt, docet esse \"profunda dei\" quae solus spiritus dei scrutatur, nobis autem deus ipse revelavit per spiritum suum, ut fide credamus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p175","Concerning Mysteries","And the evangelist John writes concerning such deep things of God: \"No man has seen God at any time; the only-begotten Son who is in the bosom of the Father, He has declared Him.\" And this same only-begotten testifies: \"No one knows the Son except the Father; nor does anyone know the Father except the Son, and he to whom the Son wills to reveal Him.\"","Et evangelista Ioannes de huiusmodi profundis dei scribit: \"deum nemo vidit umquam; unigenitus filius qui est in sinu patris, ipse enarravit.\" Hic que ipse unigenitus testatur: \"nemo novit filium nisi pater, neque patrem quis novit nisi filius, et cui voluerit filius revelare.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p176","Concerning Mysteries","Wherefore these more hidden dogmas remain by their nature above the grasp of reason, even after they have been revealed and believed by the assent of faith, and they pertain to the wisdom of God in a mystery which is hidden, because they so far exceed all that we can think or know by the light of reason alone as they exceed all that we are.","Quapropter reconditiora haec dogmata ex natura sua manent supra captum rationis, etiam postquam revelata sunt et fidei assensu credita, ac pertinent ad dei sapientiam in mysterio quae abscondita est, quoniam sic excedunt omne quod solo lumine rationis cogitare aut sapere possumus, sicut excedunt omne quod sumus."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p177","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","According to this catholic doctrine concerning the mysteries of faith, we reprove and condemn the opinion by which it is said that human reason, rightly cultivated, can of its own powers and from its own natural principles attain to understanding and demonstrating all revealed truths, and therefore that all these truths are the proper object of human philosophy.","Secundum hanc catholicam de mysteriis fidei doctrinam, reprobamus et damnamus sententiam qua dicitur rationem humanam rite excultam pertingere posse ad omnes veritates revelatas ex suis viribus suis que principiis naturalibus intelligendas ac demonstrandas, atque ideo easdem veritates omnes esse proprium obiectum humanae philosophiae."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p178","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","To divine revelation faith responds, a virtue certainly supernatural, by which through divine grace, because of the authority of God speaking, we believe that those things which are divinely revealed are true.","Revelationi divinae fides respondet, virtus nimirum supernaturalis, qua per divinam gratiam, propter auctoritatem dei loquentis, credimus vera esse quae divinitus revelata sunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p179","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","This faith is praised by the apostle Paul in the Christians, writing to the Thessalonians: \"When you received from us the word of the hearing of God, you received it not as the word of men, but as it is indeed the word of God, who works in you who have believed.\"","Hanc fidem in christianis laudat Paulus apostolus thessalonicensibus scribens: \"cum accepissetis a nobis verbum auditus dei, accepistis illud non ut verbum hominum, sed sicut est vere verbum dei, qui operatur in vobis qui credidistis.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p180","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","And by the same apostle the apostleship is commended through Jesus Christ for the obedience of faith in all nations, and thence it is said: \"Faith comes from hearing, and hearing by the word of Christ.\"","Et ab eodem apostolo commendatur apostolatus per Iesum Christum ad obediendum fidei in omnibus gentibus, atque inde dicitur: \"fides ex auditu, auditus autem per verbum Christi.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p181","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","Wherefore it is to be held by all the children of the catholic church that for the excellent virtue of faith, by which we are called and are faithful, and by which assent is to be given because of the authority of God who reveals, that persuasion of the human mind concerning truths pertaining to God and religion which arises from the intrinsic truth of the things perceived by the natural light of reason does not suffice; for such persuasion cannot be considered properly called faith, without which it is impossible to please God, lest all things be disturbed which ought to remain most firm concerning the distinction between human knowledge and divine faith.","Quare ab omnibus catholicae ecclesiae filiis tenendum imprimis est ad excellentem virtutem fidei, qua fideles nominamur et sumus, et qua propter auctoritatem dei revelantis assensus praestandus est, non sufficere illam mentis humanae persuasionem de veritatibus ad deum et ad religionem spectantibus quae ex intrinsecus perspecta rerum veritate per naturale lumen rationis oriatur; huiusmodi enim persuasio fides proprie dicta, sine qua impossibile est placere deo, censeri nequit, quin omnia perturbentur quae firmissima manere debent de distinctione inter humanam scientiam et fidem divinam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p182","Concerning Faith and Its Relation to Reason (continued)","Wherefore we condemn by anathema the doctrine if anyone says that divine faith is not distinguished from natural science which has moral or religious truth for its object, and therefore that for the act of faith by which we are called and are faithful, it is not required that revealed truth be believed because of the authority of God the revealer.","Quapropter anathemate damnamus doctrinam, si quis dixerit fidem divinam non distingui a scientia naturali quae veritatem moralem aut religiosam pro obiecto habeat, ac propterea ad actum fidei quo fideles nominamur et sumus non requiri ut revelata veritas propter auctoritatem dei revelatoris credatur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p183","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","Although, however, the faith by which we are called and are faithful is not from the intrinsic truth of the thing believed, it is nevertheless a reasonable obedience, because the revealing God has made His testimonies exceedingly credible.","Quamvis autem fides qua fideles nominamur et sumus non sit ex intrinsecus perspecta veritate rei creditae, ea tamen est rationabile obsequium, quoniam revelator deus testimonia sua credibilia fecit nimis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p184","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","For He did not institute such an order of His revelation that men should be led to faith by internal instinct alone, but with the internal helps of the Holy Spirit He willed that there should be present external motives of credibility, as certain arguments of divine revelation.","Neque enim talem instituit suae revelationis ordinem quo homines interno dumtaxat instinctu ad fidem perducerentur, sed cum internis spiritus sancti auxiliis externa credibilitatis motiva, veluti certa revelationis divinae argumenta, praesto esse voluit."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p185","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","For thus our Savior, when He gave the command to His apostles to go into the whole world and preach the gospel to every creature, sent them in virtue and in the Holy Spirit; and they, going forth, preached everywhere, the Lord working with them and confirming the word with signs that followed, by which it might be established that God had truly spoken.","Ita namque salvator noster, cum mandatum suis apostolis daret ut in universum mundum proficiscentes omni creaturae evangelium praedicarent, in virtute et in spiritu sancto eosdem misit; illi autem profecti praedicaverunt ubique, domino cooperante et sermonem confirmante sequentibus signis, quibus constaret deum esse vere loquutum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p186","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","According to the gospel of Jesus Christ itself, therefore, and according to the preaching of the apostles, it is to be held that certain signs for discerning divine revelation have been disposed by God Himself, and that to these signs, according to the order instituted by God, belong divine facts which are external and knowable even before faith is received, as signs of divine speech.","Secundum ipsum igitur evangelium Iesu Christi et secundum praedicationem apostolorum, tenendum est notas aliquas ad dignoscendam divinam revelationem a deo ipso esse dispositas, atque ad has notas, iuxta ordinem a deo institutum, pertinere facta divina quae externa sint et hominibus etiam ante fidem susceptam cognoscibilia, tanquam signa divinae loquutionis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p187","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","For human reason, while it has not yet attained to true faith, in order that it may not be deceived and err in so important a matter, ought diligently to inquire into the fact of divine revelation, and then, knowing God the revealer, all difficulty and doubt being utterly cast aside, render the full obedience of faith to the same supreme majesty and infinite truth.","Humana enim ratio, dum ad veram fidem nondum pervenit, ne in tanti momenti negotio decipiatur et erret, divinae revelationis factum diligenter inquirat oportet, ut certo sibi constet deum esse loquutum; tum vero cognoscens deum revelatorum, quavis difficultate et dubitatione penitus abiecta, plenam eidem supremae maiestati et infinitae veritati praestet fidei obedientiam."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p188","Concerning the Credibility of Divine Revelation","Wherefore we condemn by anathema the opinion of those who affirm that it cannot be that revealed truth be made credible by external signs, and therefore that men ought to be moved to faith by the internal experience of the soul alone.","Quare sub anathemate damnamus eorum sententiam qui affirmant non posse fieri ut veritas revelata signis externis reddatur credibilis, atque ideo sola interna animi experientia homines ad fidem moveri debere."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p189","Concerning the Nature of Faith As a Supernatural Virtue","Since, moreover, some, renewing an error already long ago condemned by the church, have in these last times affirmed that faith itself, by which the revealed truths are to be believed, is a natural conviction of reason induced by necessary arguments according to the laws of human science, so that faith of itself, unless it be already living and operating through charity, is not to be ascribed to supernatural grace nor is it a free virtue, nor even mediately salutary, therefore, adhering to divine revelation according to the declarations of the preceding councils and of the holy fathers, we again teach and define that faith itself in itself, by which we are faithful, is a gift of God, supernatural, the beginning, foundation, and root of all justification, and therefore that this faith itself and the assent of faith is a work of divine grace.","Quoniam praeterea nonnulli errorem ab ecclesia iam pridem damnatum postremis his temporibus renovaverunt affirmantes fidem ipsam, qua credendae sunt veritates revelatae, rationis convictionem esse naturalem necessariis argumentis inductam secundum leges scientiae humanae, ita ut fides per se, nisi iam viva sit et per charitatem operetur, nec gratiae supernaturali adscribenda nec virtus sit libera et ne mediate quidem salutaris, propterea inhaerentes divinae revelationi, secundum praecedentium conciliorum et sanctorum patrum declarationes, iterum docemus et definimus fidem ipsam in se, qua fideles sumus, donum dei esse supernaturale, humanae salutis initium, fundamentum et radicem omnis iustificationis, et proinde hanc ipsam fidem fidei que assensum esse opus divinae gratiae."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p190","Concerning the Nature of Faith As a Supernatural Virtue","For with the external proposition of faith, the internal grace of illumination and inspiration of the Holy Spirit, who gives to all sweetness in consenting and believing, is necessary that the act of faith may be as it ought, that is, supernatural and pertaining to the way of salvation.","Cum externa enim fidei propositione, gratia interna illuminationis et inspirationis spiritus sancti, qui dat omnibus suavitatem in consentiendo et credendo, necessaria est, ut actus fidei sit sicut oportet, hoc est supernaturalis et pertinens ad viam salutis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p191","Concerning the Nature of Faith As a Supernatural Virtue","But just as the acts of the virtues of which faith is the foundation remain under the help of grace, so God wills even the free obedience of faith in consenting to the truth.","Sicut autem actus virtutum quarum fides fundamentum est sub adiutorio gratiae manent, ita vult deus etiam liberam obedientiam fidei in consentiendo veritati."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p192","Concerning the Nature of Faith As a Supernatural Virtue","We are admonished of this freedom in the precepts of faith, while we are commanded to believe the truths, and pertinacious unbelief against the sufficient proposition of revelation is condemned; for \"not all obey the gospel,\" and \"he who does not believe is already judged.\"","Huius libertatis admonemur in praeceptis fidei, dum iubemur credere veritates, et pertinax contra sufficientem propositionem revelationis incredulitas damnatur; non enim \"omnes obediunt evangelio,\" et \"qui non credit iam iudicatus est.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p193","Concerning the Nature of Faith As a Supernatural Virtue","Wherefore we declare and condemn as heretical the opinion if anyone says that this faith by which Christians consent to the gospel preaching, unless it be already living faith which operates through charity, is not a supernatural gift of God, but a natural and necessary persuasion of human reason.","Quare haereticam esse declaramus et damnamus sententiam, si quis dixerit hanc fidem qua christiani evangelicae praedicationi consentiunt, nisi sit iam fides viva quae per charitatem operatur, non esse donum dei supernaturale, sed esse naturalem et necessariam persuasionem rationis humanae."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p194","Concerning the Constancy of Faith","From these condemned errors there follows a most pernicious fallacy, contrived to weaken the constancy of faith, which says that it is lawful for a catholic man, after faith has already been acknowledged and received under the magisterium of the catholic church, to call that same faith into real doubt and to suspend his assent, that he may more freely examine the foundations and motives of faith according to the norm of reason and human science, so that in this examination the condition of catholic faithful ought to be equal to that of others who are engaged in a heterodox religion.","Ex damnatis his erroribus consequitur funestissima fallacia ad labefactandam fidei constantiam composita, qua dicitur homini catholico post fidem sub magisterio catholicae ecclesiae iam agnitam et susceptam fas esse fidem eandem in reale dubium vocare, suum que assensum suspendere, quo liberius fundamenta ac motiva fidei secundum normam rationis et scientiae humanae examinet, ita ut in hoc examine par esse debeat conditio fidelium catholicorum ac aliorum qui in religione heterodoxa versantur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p195","Concerning the Constancy of Faith","Let the doctrine of the necessity of divine faith and of the duty of unchangeable constancy in the same be always before the eyes of all the faithful, that they may beware of this most pernicious error.","Ab huiusmodi perniciosissimo errore ut fideles omnes sibi caveant, doctrina catholica de necessitate divinae fidei et de officio incommutabilis in eadem constantiae perpetuo eis ob oculos versetur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p196","Concerning the Constancy of Faith","The reception of true faith and the constant profession of the same is altogether necessary for justice before God and for eternal salvation, because \"without faith it is impossible to please God,\" and the sentence of our Savior, who commanded His gospel to be preached to every creature, remains: \"He who does not believe shall be condemned.\"","Verae fidei susceptio nimirum et eiusdem constans professio ad iustitiam apud deum et ad salutem aeternam omnino necessaria est, quoniam \"sine fide impossibile est placere deo,\" et salvatoris nostri, qui iussit evangelium suum praedicari omni creaturae, manet sententia: \"qui non crediderit condemnabitur.\""],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p197","Concerning the Constancy of Faith","Hence those unhappy ones who, having once been illuminated and tasted the good word of God, have fallen away, are compared by the apostle to the reprobate earth which is near a curse, and whose consummation is unto burning.","Hinc eo etiam magis illi infelices qui semel illuminati sunt, gustaverunt que bonum dei verbum et prolapsi sunt, rursum crucifigentes sibimet ipsis filium dei et ostentui habentes, comparantur ab apostolo terrae reprobae, quae maledictioni proxima est, et cuius consummatio in combustionem."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p198","Concerning the Constancy of Faith","So that men may be able to satisfy this necessary obligation of embracing the true faith and of persevering constantly in it, God Himself, who insists on the precept, also goes before with help. For just as according to the order instituted by the author and finisher of our faith, for leading men into the admirable light of faith, internal grace is joined with the heard word of God, so both graces, both the external of the proposition of faith and especially the internal, aid our weakness for the firmness and constancy in the faith already received.","Ut autem huic necessariae obligationi amplectendi veram fidem et in eadem constanter perseverandi homines satisfacere valeant, deus ipse, qui instat praecepto, etiam praecurrit auxilio. Sicut enim secundum ordinem institutum a fidei nostrae auctore et consummatore ad deducendos homines in admirabile lumen fidei cum verbo auditus dei coniungitur gratia interna, ita gratia utraque, tum externa propositionis fidei, tum maxime interna, adiuvat infirmitatem nostram ad firmitatem et constantiam in fide iam suscepta."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p199","Concerning the Church As the Guardian of Revelation","The Savior of the human race wished the proposition and preaching of all revealed doctrine to be made suitable to all nations and all times by sending His legates to the ends of the earth and to the consummation of the age in perpetual succession and agreement under a visible head, distinguished by the characteristics of the divine mission, and thus He established the catholic church and distinguished it with manifest signs of His institution, that it might be recognized by all men as the guardian and teacher of the revealed word.","Propositionem et praedicationem totius doctrinae revelatae salvator humani generis voluit accommodatam reddere omnibus gentibus et omnibus temporibus, suos mittendo legatos usque ad ultimum terrae et usque ad consummationem saeculi in perpetua successione et consensione sub visibili capite, insignitos characteribus divinae missionis, atque ita catholicam ecclesiam instituit, suae que institutionis manifestis notis distinxit, ut ab omnibus hominibus agnosceretur custos et magistra verbi revelati."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p200","Concerning the Church As the Guardian of Revelation","To this alone, therefore, and to no other religious communion, pertain all the motives which have been divinely disposed for the evident credibility of the Christian faith, so many and so wonderful.","Ad hanc propterea solam et ad nullam aliam religionis communionem pertinent motiva omnia quae ad evidentem credibilitatem fidei christianae tam multa et tam mira divinitus sunt disposita."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p201","Concerning the Church As the Guardian of Revelation","Wherefore, just as this true church of Jesus Christ is to those who have not yet reached it a sign lifted up by God Himself among the nations and a perpetual admonition to seek and, when found, to embrace the truth, so in the same church, one, holy, catholic, and apostolic, the faithful, both learned and unlearned, rest on most firm foundations for the full certainty of credibility, always ready, as far as pertains to each according to his condition, to give satisfaction to everyone asking a reason of the hope that is in them.","Quocirca sicut haec vera ecclesia Iesu Christi illis qui ad eam nondum pervenerunt est signum a deo ipso levatum in nationes et perpetua admonitio ut quaerant et repertam amplectantur veritatem, ita in eadem ecclesia, una sancta catholica et apostolica, fideles, docti et indocti, fundamentis innituntur firmissimis ad plenam credibilitatis certitudinem, parati semper, quantum ad singulos pro eorum conditione spectat, ad satisfactionem omni poscenti rationem de ea quae in ipsis est spe."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p202","Concerning the Independence of Reason From Faith","But those men of our age also err dangerously who so preach the liberty of science that in cultivating all natural and rational sciences they claim for reason full independence from divine faith and from the authority of the church, to which the custody and infallible interpretation of all revealed truth has been divinely committed.","Sed periculose etiam errant nostrae aetatis homines illi qui libertatem scientiae ita praedicant ut in omnibus naturalibus et rationalibus scientiis excolendis rationi vindicent plenam independentiam ab ipsa fide divina et ab auctoritate ecclesiae, cui custodia et infallibilis interpretatio totius revelatae veritatis divinitus commissa est."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p203","Concerning the Independence of Reason From Faith","Just as the apostles and their successors to the consummation of the age have received the apostleship for the obedience of faith in all nations and to speak the wisdom of God in the doctrine of the Spirit, so it is also of the same office to proscribe knowledge falsely so called, which some promising have erred concerning the faith.","Sicut apostoli eorum que usque ad consummationem saeculi successores acceperunt apostolatum ad obediendum fidei in omnibus gentibus et ad loquendum dei sapientiam in doctrina spiritus, ita etiam eorundem muneris est proscribere falsi nominis scientiam, quam quidam promittentes circa fidem exciderunt."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p204","Concerning the Independence of Reason From Faith","For although natural science also is from God as from the supreme truth and creator of human reason, and reason itself is a most noble gift for rightly investigating and understanding from its own principles the truths proportioned to it, yet the same human reason, fallible and weak (as sad experience too much testifies), can be led to opinions opposed to the infallible doctrine of the church, which, since truth never contradicts truth, and therefore every assertion contrary to the truth of illuminated faith is altogether false, now pertains not to true but to knowledge falsely so called, nor can they have anything but a false appearance of truth.","Licet enim naturalis quoque scientia a deo sit ut a veritate suprema et humanae rationis creatore, atque ratio ipsa nobilissimum sit donum ad veritates sibi proportionatas ex suis principiis recte perscrutandas et intelligendas, potest tamen eadem ratio humana, fallibilis et infirma (ut luctuosa experientia nimium testatur), deduci ad sententias infallibili doctrinae ecclesiae adversantes, quae, cum verum vero minime contradicat, adeo que omnis assertio veritati illuminatae fidei contraria omnino falsa sit, iam non ad veram, sed ad falsi nominis scientiam necessario pertinent, nec possunt habere nisi fallacem speciem veritatis."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p205","Concerning the Independence of Reason From Faith","Therefore all the children of the church are bound by the same most holy duty by which they are bound constantly to keep the sincere faith and to follow sound doctrine, also to hold opinions contrary to catholic doctrine not as fruit or consequences of genuine science, but altogether as errors, in the manner in which they have been proscribed by the infallible judgment of the church; for it cannot be that catholic truth be retained in a faithful mind and at the same time be knowingly impugned under the name of philosophy by contrary opinions as legitimately consequent from the principles of true science.","Quare omnes filii ecclesiae eodem officio sanctissimo quo fidem sinceram constanter servare sanam que doctrinam sequi tenentur, obstringuntur etiam ut sententias catholicae doctrinae contrarias non pro fructu aut consectariis genuinae scientiae, sed omnino pro erroribus habeant, eo modo quo infallibili ecclesiae iudicio fuerint proscriptae; fieri enim nequit ut veritas catholica animo fideli retineatur, eadem que simul sub nomine philosophiae scienter impugnetur sententiis contrariis velut legitime consequentibus ex principiis verae scientiae."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p206","Concerning the Independence of Reason From Faith","By this obligation nothing is detracted from the sincere study of investigating natural truth, for not the use of science but its abuse alone is forbidden; nor is it forbidden to investigate truth and to demonstrate it from the proper principles of reason and science, but only to embrace error, and that which is opposed to faith or accommodated to weaken it.","Hac autem obligatione non detrahitur quidquam sincero veritatis naturalis investigandae studio, cum non scientiae usus sed eiusdem solus abusus interdicatur; nec veritatem indagare ac propriis rationis et scientiae principiis demonstrare, sed errorem solummodo eum que fidei adversantem vel ad illam labefactandam accommodatum amplecti prohibeatur."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p207","Concerning the Relation Between Faith and Science","We reprove, therefore, and condemn by anathema the doctrine by which it is asserted that the treatment of natural and rational sciences ought to be so much its own and wholly independent that the opinions which are established and deduced in them, even if they are repugnant to catholic doctrine, are not subject to the judgment and authentic proscription of the church, and therefore that natural and rational sciences are to be treated even with the risk of erring against faith and catholic doctrine, no account being taken of supernatural revelation.","Reprobamus itaque et anathemate damnamus doctrinam qua asseritur tractationem scientiarum naturalium et rationalium ita sui iuris et plane independentem esse oportere, ut sententiae quae in illis statuuntur et deducuntur, etiamsi doctrinae catholicae repugnent, non subsint iudicio et authenticae proscriptioni ecclesiae, atque ideo scientias naturales et rationales etiam sub discrimine in eis errandi contra fidem et doctrinam catholicam tractandas esse, nulla supernaturalis revelationis habita ratione."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p208","Concerning the Development of Dogma","From the pernicious disturbance of the legitimate relation between faith and science, besides other evils, this most grave evil happens, that rash men dare to interpret the very mysteries of revelation and the sense (which is no other than that retained and declared by the catholic church) by their own judgment, and to twist the most holy dogmas to senses abhorrent from the universal faith and the doctrine of the church, by which the revealed truths may be accommodated to the fallacious measure of the human intellect; and thus, while the words of the church are retained, alien senses are substituted, so that the same things seem to be said, yet different things are said.","Ex perniciosa turbatione legitimae relationis inter fidem ac scientiam, praeter alia mala, illud accidit gravissimum, ut homines temerarii ipsa mysteria revelationis que sensum (qui non alius est quam retentus ac declaratus ab ecclesia catholica) proprio arbitrio interpretari audeant et dogmata sanctissima detorquere ad sensus ab universali fide et ecclesiae doctrina abhorrentes, quibus revelatae veritates accommodentur fallaci mensurae intellectus humani; atque ita, dum retentis verbis ecclesiae substituuntur sensus alieni, videantur dici eadem, dicantur tamen diversa."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p209","Concerning the Development of Dogma","Nor do they fear to affirm that since the understanding of dogmas has hitherto been imperfect and could not yet be rightly perfected in the church on account of the defect of philosophy, now at length a way lies open to reason, cultivated by science, to the true and proper understanding of all revealed truths.","Nec verentur affirmare cum intelligentia dogmatum hactenus fuerit imperfecta, necdum in ecclesia propter defectum philosophiae rite ea perfici potuerit, nunc demum rationi per scientiam excultae patere viam ad verum et proprium intellectum omnium veritatum revelatarum."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p210","Concerning the Development of Dogma","But the firm foundation of God stands; for the doctrine of faith, made known by God to men for believing because of the authority of God Himself who reveals, is not, as a philosophical invention, to be perfected by human ingenuity, but is a deposit, the custody, explanation, and definition of which has been divinely entrusted to the infallible church under the assistance of the Spirit of truth; and therefore it is evident that the sense of the dogmas declared by the same church is always to be inviolably retained, and that its change is not progress of science but perversion of the heretical spirit.","Sed firmum fundamentum dei stabit; neque enim doctrina fidei a deo hominibus patefacta ad credendum propter ipsam dei revelantis auctoritatem est velut philosophicum inventum humanis ingeniis perficiendum, sed est depositum, cuius custodia, explicatio et definitio sub assistentia spiritus veritatis infallibili ecclesiae divinitus est concredita; atque ideo constat sensum dogmatum ab eadem ecclesia declaratum semper inviolabiliter esse retinendum, eius vero commutationem non profectum esse scientiae, sed haeretici spiritus perversionem."],["vol2/vatican-i/index.html","p211","Concerning the Development of Dogma","Wherefore if anyone says that such an explanation of the understanding of dogmas is to be admitted by the progress of human reason and sciences that the sense which the church in its definitions and in its proposition of the articles of faith understood and understands is not always simply and unchangeably true, but that according to the progress of sciences, a different sense is sometime to be substituted for the same definitions and proposed articles of faith: let him be anathema.","Quare si quis dixerit talem admittendam esse intelligentiae dogmatum explicationem per humanae rationis et scientiarum progressum, ut ille sensus quem ecclesia in suis definitionibus sua que articulorum fidei propositione intellexit et intelligit non semper simpliciter et incommutabiliter verus sit, sed secundum profectum scientiarum iisdem definitionibus et propositis fidei articulis aliquando alius diversus sensus substitui debeat: anathema sit."]];